\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Rupiah Taking Respite \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shen Hong \par \uc2 909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2003\par 11:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Led by the rupiah, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly higher against the U.S. dollar late Wednesday, recovering from the previous day's jitters brought on by a bomb blast in the Indonesian capital. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide against the Japanese yen and the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, move higher, observers said. \par \par \uc2  "The ability of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to withstand such an event in Jakarta is a good thing," said Thio Chin Loo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  However, Thio was quick to warn that the steadiness in regional units Wednesday "could be a bit artificial because central banks are stepping up vigilance." \par \par \uc2  The blast at a leading Jakarta hotel Tuesday killed at least 10 people and renewed security concerns across Southeast Asia. The Al-Qaida-linked Islamic militant group Jemaah Islamiyah has claimed responsibility for the bombing in Jakarta and has warned of further attacks. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at 8,608 rupiah, down from IDR8,625 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Although Bank Indonesia has vowed to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following Tuesday's plunge on the terrorist bombing attack in Jakarta, some observers expect the rupiah to resume heading south in the near future, particularly as political instability could rise ahead of Indonesia's mid-2004 presidential election. \par \par \uc2  "We are moving back to an environment where political risks are underpriced...and these sorts of event risks will be more of an issue in the run-up to the election," said James Malcolm, a strategist at JPMorgan Chase in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Despite Bank Indonesia's efforts to limit the rupiah's downside, "people will be looking to reduce exposure to Indonesia," Malcolm added. "The trend of dollar-rupiah should be up, rather than down." \par \par \uc2  Malcolm said JPMorgan Chase expects the U.S. dollar to rise gradually to IDR9,500 by the end of this year. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar also rebounded from a three-month low it hit Tuesday, taking advantage of the spillover impact of a stronger yen. The U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7622 late in the session, down from S$1.7640 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, which tends to take its cue from its Singapore counterpart, was unchanged late in Asia, following a brief rebound earlier Wednesday. The U.S. dollar was quoted flat at THB42.07, after dipping to an intraday low of THB41.96. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso also reversed direction Tuesday, capitalizing on better corporate demand and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP54.790 on the Philippine Dealing System, compared with PHP54.815 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In Northeast Asia, the South Korean won ended the day higher, as foreign investors sold dollars to pay for local stock purchases, but narrowed gains toward the close on suspected government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,184.6 won, down from KRW1,187.9 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net buyers of KRW84.3 billion worth of Korean shares Monday and Tuesday, before turning net sellers Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Market participants also suspected the authorities stepped into the market right before the close to support the dollar. The Korean authorities never specifically comment on their activities in the market. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the dollar to trade between KRW1,183 and KRW1,190 Thursday, with any downside limited by the government's clear aversion to a rising won as it tries to keep local exports competitive. \par \par \uc2  "The market's consensus is that it will be difficult for the dollar-won to fall below 1,180," said a trader at Korea Exchange Bank. \par \par \uc2  In China, Premier Wen Jiabao's reaffirmation that the country would maintain the basic stability of the yuan exchange rate led to a narrowing in the one-year yuan nondeliverable forward discount in the offshore market. \par \par \uc2  The one-year dollar NDF was quoted at a discount of 1500 to the yuan late Wednesday, compared with 1585 Tuesday. This implies that market players expect the U.S. dollar to weaken to CNY8.1273 in 12 months' time. \par \par \uc2  The yen's strength against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lifted the New Taiwan dollar, sparking a slew of dollar selling from Taiwanese domestic banks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.446, down from NT$34.466 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often tracks the yen, because the island imports large amounts of goods from Japan and as the two compete in some export categories. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar is expected to remain steady Thursday between NT$34.42\par and NT$34.47, said a dealer at a foreign bank in Taipei.\par    -By Shen Hong, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4156;\par hong.shen@dowjones.com\par    -Edited by Lim Mui Khi\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Corrected August 6, 2003 23:57 ET (03:57 GMT)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In China, Premier Wen Jiabao's reaffirmation that the country would\par maintain the basic stability of the yuan exchange rate led to a\par narrowing in the one-year yuan nondeliverable forward discount in the\par offshore market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The one-year dollar NDF was quoted at a discount of 1500 to the yuan\par late Wednesday, compared with 1585 Tuesday. This implies that market\par players expect the U.S. dollar to weaken to CNY8.1273 in 12 months'\par time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (In an item that ran at 0956 GMT Wednesday, the one-year yuan\par nondeliverable forward was incorrectly described as trading at a\par premium.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030806dz86000mw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
