\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Ctrl Bk Presence Detected \b0\par\par\uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2003\par 12:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Thursday, with traders reporting the presence of at least two central banks in the markets during the session. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso's woes continued unabated and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at yet another four-month low, marking its fourth straight day of declines since Sunday's mutiny by junior army officers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 54.720 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.700 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Despite pressure on the peso, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its closely watched overnight rates unchanged at its policy-setting meeting Thursday, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent weakening hasn't affected the country's inflation outlook and isn't interest rate-related. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and the New Taiwan dollar were also weaker, while the Indonesian rupiah was flat, and the South Korean won and Thai baht were modestly stronger. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  all kicked off the session on a weak note following the yen's overnight fall, but either narrowed or reversed losses as the yen made a mild recovery in Asia. \par \par \uc2  At 0945 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y120.17, down from Y120.27 overnight in New York but up from Y119.82 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Traders detected the presence of the central banks of Taiwan and Thailand in the markets. Some also suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the South Korean won market. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said agents of Bank of Thailand were looking to sell dollars at the 42.05-42.10 baht level. \par \par \uc2  He didn't think it was because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more amenable to the idea of a stronger baht. Rather, he said it was an exercise to smoothen volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, noting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also buys dollars when the baht firms sharply. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around 41.94 baht, down from THB41.97 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won reversed direction, tracking the yen's mild recovery, and aided also by dollar sales from local exporters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which opened at the intraday high of KRW1,182.0, finished at KRW1,179.7, slightly down from KRW1,180.0 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Exporter dollar sales were heavier than expected," a dealer at the Industrial Bank of Korea said. "They must have been waiting for the dollar to pick up (to sell dollars)." \par \par \uc2  But investors were too cautious to test further downside for fear of attracting government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some traders in Seoul suspected the government provided limited support for the dollar indirectly during the session. \par \par \uc2  "It seemed that the government was in smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...absorbing some of (the) dollar sales," said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. unit ended at NT$34.417, up from Wednesday's NT$34.400. \par \par \uc2  Taipei dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  low in line with the yen to maintain the local dollar's competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar generally follows the yen as Taiwan and Japan compete in several export categories. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7593 late in Asia, up from S$1.7558 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A European bank dealer in Singapore said there was a lot of activity in cross trades on the local dollar, notably against the Thai baht and the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Investors were selling the U.S. dollar/baht pair to buy the U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar pair because they see limited upside in the former, he said. \par \par \uc2  "This (cross trades) is probably the trades to put on in the next few sessions," he said. \par \par \uc2  The release of preliminary unemployment numbers for the second quarter didn't create any major market reaction, though some dealers said the data generally reflected the still-uncertain outlook for the local economy. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's unemployment rate was surprisingly steady in the second quarter, but a closer look at the data revealed a continued worsening of the job market, economists said. \par \par \uc2  The seasonally adjusted jobless rate stayed at 4.5% as of end-June, unchanged from the end of the first quarter, although more people lost their jobs as the economy shrank 4.3% because of SARS and weak global conditions. The jobless rate held steady only because of people exiting the work force and various seasonal factors. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah finished steady in a choppy session as dollar selling by foreign investors offset month-end dollar demand from local companies, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed unchanged at IDR8,510. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, dollar sales by foreign investors who had to settle their purchases of Bank Danamon shares Monday drove the U.S. unit to an intraday low of IDR8,475, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency sold a 20% stake in Bank Danamon to raise cash for the state budget. Almost half shares - worth around IDR1.3 trillion - were sold to foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said most speculators chose to stay on the sidelines ahead of the 10-day annual convention of the nation's highest legislature, which begins Friday. \par \par \uc2  Some market players expect the session to be dominated by rising political tensions, although these aren't likely to threaten Megawati Sukarnoputri's presidency. \par \par \uc2  The market is also concerned the session could be tarnished by terrorism, recalling the bomb blast at the parliament building earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Mary de Wet \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030731dz7v000sq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
