\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late, Peso Trims Early Losses \b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2003\par 10:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Philippine peso clawed out of intraday troughs Monday but remained at a four-month low following a failed mutiny over the weekend. But regional counterparts barely reacted to the incident. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and traders viewed the mutiny by junior soldiers in the Philippines Sunday as an event that was specific to that country, with little danger of contagion. \par \par \uc2  "The coup in the Philippine is very much a domestic issue, opposed to, say, a terrorist act, which has wider implications for the region. Hence, we expect any spillover impact to be limited," BNP Paribas wrote in a late note Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 54.380 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.010 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar kicked off the session at PHP54.500 and rose as high as PHP54.600 before retreating. It failed to hit the PHP55 level some had predicted earlier. \par \par \uc2  Traders and analysts said the peso wasn't badly mauled because the mutiny was resolved quickly and peacefully. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also saved from a free-fall by the fact that foreign investment in the Philippines has already been limited by economic and security concerns. As a result, there was no rush by foreign investors to sell their peso holdings. \par \par \uc2  "That's why the peso didn't weaken big time," said Bhanu Baweja, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS AG in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the peso during the session. \par \par \uc2  Generally, analysts expect Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep to a strengthening bias against the U.S. dollar over the course of the year as they remain bearish on the U.S. unit. But the peso's gains will likely be more modest as topside is capped by economic and security issues, they said. \par \par \uc2  The PHP (peso) will remain on the defensive in the near-term, but a dramatic collapse is unlikely," BNP Paribas said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely defend the PHP55.00 level vigorously, the French investment bank added. \par \par \uc2  This week, investors will be closely watching a slew of U.S. data for confirmation of whether the economy has indeed begun to improve. \par \par \uc2  It will also be an important week for data in Asia. Exports data from South Korea due Friday, for instance, "will provide the first hints of how the region's exports are holding up after the impressive performance in June," Bank of America said in a note. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean won, the dollar closed at 1,178.8, a tad below Friday's KRW1,180.1, due to selling by local exporters and foreign investors in local shares, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The selling wasn't aggressive and overall trading was slow. \par \par \uc2  "The market was in for another quiet session. The global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a whole remains slow," said Koo Sang-Jun, a dealer at KorAm Bank. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, weaker-than-expected manufacturing output data for June pulled the Singapore dollar off earlier highs, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7480, down from S$1.7488 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Development Board Monday said industrial production fell 7.3% on year in June, marking a third straight month of contraction. The drop was worse than the average 4.3% decline forecast in a Dow Jones Newswires poll. \par \par \uc2  Higher share prices and U.S. dollar selling by exporters helped lift the New Taiwan dollar, but gains were partly offset by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit closed at NT$34.388, down from NT$34.405 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Trading was relatively quiet and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair was confined to a narrow range during the session after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called some bank traders last week to express concern over their short U.S. dollar positions, said a Taipei-based dealer. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume totaled US$236 million, down from US$260 million in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah gave up early gains to close slightly lower on month-end dollar demand from importers, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed the local session at 8,530 rupiah, edging up from IDR8,520 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar earlier slid to a low of IDR8,480 as investors sold back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its recent rise to three-month highs. \par \par \uc2  But local importers, led by state-owned oil and gas company Pertamina, snapped up the dollar to finance their imports, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking on rupiah-denominated assets, which was responsible for the dollar's recent rally, appeared to have died out. \par \par \uc2  "It seemed to be only temporary and most foreign investors are still comfortable in parking their funds in Indonesia," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "There is no negative news on Indonesia as macroeconomic pictures continue improving although slowly." \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency said Monday it has sold another 20% stake in Bank Danamon since Friday, raising IDR1.3 trillion. Although most of the shares were sold to local investors, the offer drew strong interest from foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also gave up early gains and moved to a slightly softer footing. Late in Asia, the U.S. dollar was quoted around THB41.98, slightly up from THB41.95 Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Mary de Wet \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030728dz7s000kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
