\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Foreign Banks Unwind Long USDs \b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2003\par 10:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was firmer late Thursday as foreign banks trimmed long U.S. dollar positions in the face of the U.S. unit's broader retreat versus regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0810 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7495, down from S$1.7562 late in Asia Wednesday. It traded between S$1.7490 and S$1.7560 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower around S$1.7550 following its retreat against the euro and yen overnight. It initially held steady during a lackluster morning session, but gradually drifted lower as foreign banks unwound their long positions as other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended their early gains. \par \par \uc2  "It was persistent but gradual (selling). Some offshore names sold from around S$1.7520 and pushed it (the U.S. dollar) further down," said an Asian bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Around midafternoon, the U.S. dollar's retreat was partly accelerated by its slide against the baht after the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said a recent easing of restrictions on foreign investment by some local institutions wasn't aimed at weakening the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The changes, which included allowing exporters to hold onto their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings for longer periods, were to allow capital flows and to let \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate stabilize, Bank of Thailand said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar-baht fall seems to have prompted more dollar-Sing selling," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar's fall was stemmed just before the Asian session ended as selling momentum eased. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, dealers expect the U.S. dollar to stay between S$1.7480 and S$1.7530. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended lower Thursday, reversing early gains that followed an overnight rise in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Selling in local bonds returned after a two-day respite amid speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday to buy 10-year bonds in an attempt to add liquidity and ease volatility in the local bond market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have focused their buying in 10-year bonds following a recent sell-off in longer-dated maturities. \par \par \uc2  At the end of Thursday's session, the yield on two-year bonds was up one basis point at 0.94%, while the yield on five-year bonds rose two basis points to 2.02%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was two basis points higher at 2.60%, while the yield on 10-year bonds gained three basis points to 3.09%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds rose two basis points to 3.44%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the MAS' bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, offers were also mostly higher as\par they tracked local bond yields. The two-year offer slipped two basis\par points to 1.15%, while the five-year swap offer slipped one basis point\par to 2.14%. The 10-year offer gained two basis points to close at 3.18%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 203 basis points\par from 199 basis points late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mixed though liquidity was still\par ample in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.3750% 0.5000%   0.1875% 0.2500%\par 3-Month           0.5625% 0.6875%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par 1-Year            0.6875% 0.8125%   0.7500% 0.8750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Kevin Lim\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030724dz7o000ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
