\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Bonds-Singapore yields soar in growth-inspired rout. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2003\par 10:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 22 (Reuters) - Singapore bond yields lurched to nine-month highs on Tuesday as government bond prices across Asia fell in a sell-off driven by tumbling U.S. Treasuries as investors embrace signs of improving U.S. growth. \par \par \uc2  Yields could rise further if economic numbers continue to improve, raising hopes of a global turnaround and fuelling expectations of higher inflation, but fixed income analysts said it was premature to call an end to the Asian bond rally. \par \par \uc2  Treasuries have fallen markedly since late June, after a smaller than expected rate cut from U.S. Federal Reserve, and the losses gathered pace last week after Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan spoke of improving growth in Asia's biggest export market. \par \par \uc2  "The notion of a U.S. and global economic recovery has spooked bond markets in the trade-oriented Asian economies," said Vasan Shridharan, regional economist with HSBC. \par \par \uc2  The carnage in Singapore led 10-year yields to nine-month highs of 3.45 percent on Tuesday, in a steep ascent from historic lows of 1.78 in mid-May, before easing slightly. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, ten-year yields have risen 70 basis points to 2.1 percent this week. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore is highly geared to the external world, hence its bonds are underperforming. Leverage tends to cut you both ways," Shridharan said. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's volatility raised speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit the fall in bond prices. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumours. In an e-mailed response to Reuters, it said it would use various instruments as part of its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the outright purchase and sale of government bonds, to ensure there was sufficient liquidity in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Singapore's sell-off had been more severe than in the United States, where 10-year yields have leapt 110 basis points since mid-June. \par \par \uc2  Shridharan said the return on 10-year U.S. bonds had been a negative eight percent since mid-June. \par \par \uc2  But Asian central banks, sitting on a pile of investments in U.S. Treasuries whose value is being eroded, would not be griping too much, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Some of the Treasury holdings have been built up as central banks around the region try to limit their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains against the U.S. dollar to protect export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "They would be happy to withstand the losses if they have a global upturn and weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Simon Klassen, fixed income strategist with JP Morgan. \par \par \uc2  SUSPICIONS ABOUT GROWTH \par \par \uc2  Implicit in the steepening of most of bond curves, including the United States where the spread between 10 and two-year bonds is at decade highs, was a tenuous view that long term rates will rise as stronger growth brought inflation, analysts said. But that may be overly upbeat on Asia. Singapore has just posted its biggest ever contraction in quarterly growth, Korea's economy is in recession - and data is expected to show Hong Kong is also. Much of the region is also struggling against deflation. \par \par \uc2  Barclays Capital said in a note to clients the fall in bond prices provided a buying opportunity and the high correlation between Asian bonds and U.S. Treasuries would soon snap. \par \par \uc2  Asia's tight fiscal policies, still rampant disinflation, abundant liquidity and the possibility of further rate cuts in places such as Thailand, Korea and Indonesia, would mean an interest rates rally in the region still has legs, it said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, analysts said investors were selling bonds and holding cash but not quite ready to invest in risky equities. \par \par \uc2  And they advised those still holding their bond investments to switch to the short end, which would limit damages from a sell-off and guarantee some benefit if official rates were cut to try to spur economic activity. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030722dz7m00gde\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
