\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; Greenspan Remarks Lift Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2003\par 12:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened along with the yen Wednesday after the U.S. dollar received a leg-up from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's upbeat assessment of the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, what happened overnight after Greenspan (addressed the House Financial Services Committee) gave the dollar a bit of a boost. All the dollar/Asians reacted the same," said a trader at a European bank in Singapore. He was referring to the U.S. dollar's rally against major rivals. \par \par \uc2  "Everything (the dollar versus Asian units) just gapped up," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  Traders suspected Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed through on overnight yen-selling activities in Asia, which helped pushed the dollar above the Y118 mark. \par \par \uc2  At 1001 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y118.30, up from Y117.79 overnight in New York and Y117.69 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks in the region, traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The European bank trader said, however, that some market players began to sell the dollar at its peak against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is some topside selling coming in. People are looking to sell," he said, noting that investors were becoming cautions over how the dollar will move later in the global session. \par \par \uc2  Given the U.S. Treasury sell-off overnight, "It will be interesting to see if Greenspan changes his tone or language in the session (later Wednesday) to the Senate banking committee in an attempt to dampen the rise in yields," Goldman Sachs said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won snapped a six-session winning streak and fell in tandem with the yen, though it pared most of the initial losses due to heavy dollar sales by foreign stock investors and exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose as high as 1,183.0 won but closed at the intraday low of KRW1,176.7 won, only slightly up from KRW1,176.0 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Local markets will be closed for a holiday Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended the session at its weakest in almost two weeks as interbank players covered short positions on the U.S. unit following Greenspan's remarks. \par \par \uc2  Importers were also buyers of the U.S. dollar, which ended at NT$34.410, up from NT$34.364 Tuesday, marking its highest close since July 3. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers said demand for the local dollar remained solid as the key stock index rose 1.5% to a one-year high Wednesday. Foreign investors were net buyers of NT$4.6 billion worth of local shares during the session. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said anticipation of continued foreign inflows into the stock market gives an upward bias to the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7612 late in Asia, up from S$1.7554 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Most market participants said they viewed the current U.S. dollar level against the local unit to be near the midpoint of the monetary authorities policy band for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore guides the Singapore dollar within an undisclosed band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit's overnight gains against major rivals sparked off dollar buying in the Jakarta interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to an intraday high of IDR8,250 before ending the local session at IDR8,235, up from IDR8,225 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the underlying bullish sentiment for the rupiah remains intact due to Indonesia's improving macroeconomic outlook. \par \par \uc2  Also, the interest-rate differential between the rupiah and dollar remains high despite Bank Indonesia cutting its benchmark rate Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the benchmark weighted average rate on the one-month Sertifikat bank Indonesia notes to 9.21% at its weekly auction, from 9.23% last week. \par \par \uc2  The one-month rupiah interest rate at banks in Jakarta is now around 9.2%, while the dollar interest rate for the same maturity is 0.95%, which makes it attractive for traders to hold rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The weaker yen and rising demand for dollars from importers pulled the Philippine peso down, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Renewed security concerns following the escape of three detained terrorists also bolstered the dollar, which ended at 53.630 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.585 in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted at THB41.65 late in Asia, up from THB41.545 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The baht's losses followed Greenspan's remarks overnight, which helped boost the greenback against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a Bangkok-based trader. \par \par \uc2  Selling by offshore banks and exporters pulled the dollar off highs, traders said. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey & Ryan Woo \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030716dz7g000nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
