\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late;Some Central Banks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 989 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2003\par 11:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a mixed bag late Tuesday at the tail end of a generally lackluster session in which some central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap strength in their units, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and Philippine peso were weaker, the Indonesian rupiah and South Korean won were little changed and the New Taiwan dollar was stronger. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was firmer compared with pre-weekend levels but was lower from offshore levels Monday, when local markets were shut for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  Monetary authorities in Thailand, Taiwan and South Korea were those suspected of buying dollars in Tuesday's session. \par \par \uc2  Traders said these central banks were in the market to smooth the movements of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than attempt to reverse the strengthening trend. \par \par \uc2  Strong portfolio inflows into regional stock markets are a key factor supporting Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But policy-makers in Asia are reluctant to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen too much for fear of losing export competitiveness amid a still uncertain outlook for the global economy, especially the U.S. \par \par \uc2  They are also unlikely to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise significantly as long as China resists pressure to revalue the yuan, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Overall, Tuesday's session mirrored that of the previous day as many investors kept to the sidelines to hear what Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan has to say about the U.S. economy later in the global session. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan will address the House Financial Services Committee Tuesday and the Senate Banking Committee Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen didn't provide much of a lead for its counterparts in the region as market players shrugged off early sharp gains in Tokyo share prices and the Bank of Japan's decision to keep monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "There's nothing happening on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  front," in the run-up to Greenspan's address, a European bank trader in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "There's some portfolio inflows" into regional stock markets, which are weighing on the dollar, and "the central banks are buying it (the dollar) up," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,176.0 against the South Korean won, a touch below Monday's KRW1,176.3, but off the intraday trough of KRW1,174.6. This was the won's sixth straight session of gains. \par \par \uc2  There was suspicion the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar when it fell to the intraday low on continued foreign buying of local shares, but traders had mixed views about this. \par \par \uc2  "Suspicious - but I don't think so. It was not that aggressive," a Seoul-based trader said, although a Singapore-based trader was sure the Bank of Korea was in the market. \par \par \uc2  Another Seoul-based trader said the dollar was also supported by news that a National Assembly committee approved a KRW4-trillion-won increase in this year's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bond issue limit. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. unit ended at NT$34.364, slightly down from NT$34.376 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said demand from exporters and inflows of funds from foreign investors buying local stocks buoyed the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  But dollar purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from importers tempered the local unit's rise, they said. \par \par \uc2  Falling volumes suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't very aggressive, a Taipei-based dealer said. Trading volume dropped for the third straight session Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened after a listless session as investors were reluctant to take strong positions ahead of Greenspan's address. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7553 late in the local session, up from S$1.7540 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said its current neutral monetary policy stance will be appropriate to keep inflation in check and support the economy for the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was steady as dollar demand from local importers offset selling by foreign investors buying into local stocks and bonds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 8,223 rupiah, virtually unchanged from IDR8,225 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Continued capital inflows showed that foreign investors are undeterred by a small bomb blast at the Indonesian Parliament building Monday. There were no casualties in the incident. \par \par \uc2  "The market seemed to have fully forgotten Monday's blast," a Jakarta-based dealer with a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was weighed down by seasonal demand for the dollar from importers, and also security concerns following the escape Monday of three suspected terrorists from a Manila prison, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 53.585 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.545 Monday, but off the intraday high of PHP53.635. \par \par \uc2  The peso trimmed some losses on news of the Supreme Court ruling in favor of the government on the disputed Swiss deposits of late president Ferdinand Marcos, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Supreme Court Tuesday forfeited in favor of the government the frozen Swiss deposits, which had increased to $658.2 million early last year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the court's ruling "would be good not only for the peso, but for the government budget as well." \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted around THB41.54 late in Asia, up from THB41.50 in offshore trading Monday, but down from THB41.64 at Friday's local close. \par \par \uc2  Traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Bank of Thailand \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the session to cap the baht's strength, which came amid a rise in Thai share prices. \par \par \uc2  "Bank of Thailand (was) squeezing some shorts (on the dollar), but essentially, the range and volume was small and thin," a European bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  A trader at another bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars at the THB41.52 to THB41.53 levels. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey & Ryan Woo \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030715dz7f000lk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
