\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; Talk of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2003\par 12:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly lower Friday in a typically subdued pre-weekend session. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar experienced the greatest swing as it lost more ground after a choppy ride in the previous session following the de facto loosening of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7568, up from S$1.7510 previously. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar and the Philippine peso were clustered on the weak side of late Thursday's levels. The South Korean won, Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah were little changed. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to support the dollar as central banks in the region stand ready to act against any sharp appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of losing export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Left to themselves, economic fundamentals would justify broadly stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may even prompt protectionist measures from the U.S. and Europe, warned Bhanu Baweja, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "That's the real risk," he said. \par \par \uc2  However, "I do think that combined with a little bit of political pressure and also better equity environment, they will dilute the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, interbank players were forced to cover short U.S. dollar positions as Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to aggressively buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit ended at NT$34.400, up from Thursday's NT$34.341. Volume shrank to US$552.5 million from US$1.051 billion previously. \par \par \uc2  A local bank trader in Taipei estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought about US$200 million during the session, mostly during morning trade. \par \par \uc2  An outflow of funds to cover oil purchases by local oil importers also added to the U.S. dollar's strength, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the dollar ended at 1,178.2 won, just a tad below Thursday's KRW1,178.4. \par \par \uc2  "There is a great deal of dollars in the market on foreign stock purchases and exporters' dollar sales," said a Seoul-based trader. \par \par \uc2  The government didn't actively step into the market to defend the U.S. dollar, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Philippine peso ended slightly lower as banks bought dollars ahead of an expected rise in demand next week, though trading was largely range-bound amid a lack of fresh news, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 53.44 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.41 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Volume thinned to $90 million from $161 million previously as banks chose to hold on to their dollars on expectation of better rates next week when importers buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to service their requirements. \par \par \uc2  As against the Indonesian rupiah, the dollar closed at 8,218, barely moved from Thursday's IDR8,220. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said most market players stayed on the sidelines ahead of the weekend, amid a lack of fresh leads. \par \par \uc2  "I didn't get any calls at all today (Friday)," a dealer with a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also little changed against the Thai baht and was quoted at 41.64 late in Asia, compared with THB41.645 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Bernice Tang \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030711dz7b000me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
