\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Singapore Eases Policy, Weakening SGD As GDP Sinks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shen Hong and Izham Ahmad \par \uc2 1,154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2003\par 09:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Singapore effectively eased monetary policy Thursday as it acknowledged SARS and the Iraq war had hammered the city-state's economy more severely than expected in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  The government said gross domestic product shrank 4.3% in the three months through June from a year earlier, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore slightly altered policy to allow a one-time weakening of the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  The MAS "took the opportunity to be a bit accommodating," said economist David Cohen at MMS International. The policy tweak is a "recognition of the fact" that the economy took "quite a blow" in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  The GDP fall, the first in five quarters, was worse than the 2.3% contraction forecast by 11 economists in a Dow Jones Newswires survey, showing the harsh impact on tourism and trade of the outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome and the war. The island nation's economy grew 1.6% on year in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's advance GDP data mark Asia's first official report on economic performance covering the quarter that SARS ravaged what had been the world's liveliest economic region. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan and Hong Kong, two trade- and services-driven economies also severely affected by the deadly virus, will report second-quarter data in August. \par \par \uc2  But on the policy front, Singapore is clearly playing catch-up. Central banks in Taiwan, Thailand and Indonesia have all recently cut interest rates, as did the Bank of Korea on Thursday. In Hong Kong, the local dollar's peg to the falling U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased monetary conditions in the city and made its companies more price-competitive abroad. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a semiannual policy review lowered its target for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by "re-centering" its trading band for the unit around the current exchange rate - which it is acknowledged was near the bottom of its unpublished trading band. \par \par \uc2  "By re-centering the band and keeping the same width of the band, they are lowering the potential low point for the Singapore dollar on a trade-weighted basis, and that is easier policy," said Bhanu Baweja, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                 Cheaper SGD OK\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a country where the economic impact of trade flows dwarfs the\par amount of activity in the tiny domestic economy, the MAS regulates\par policy via the exchange rate, rather than interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It targets the Singapore dollar within an undisclosed range -\par generally thought to be plus or minus 1% to 2% - of an undisclosed,\par trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push\par up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar when it wants to control inflation or an\par overheating economy and sells the local unit when the economy needs a\par boost.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The MAS said the Singapore dollar's "nominal effective exchange rate,"\par after starting 2003 nearer the top of its trading range, had slipped in\par the past two months or so to the "the lower half of the policy band."\par This weaker exchange rate "is conducive to supporting the incipient\par recovery in the Singapore economy and to maintaining domestic price\par stability in a low-inflation environment," the MAS said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar tumbled on the news that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will\par tolerate a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but later crawled back from its lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar spiked 0.7% to S$1.7608 from around S$1.7480 before\par the GDP and MAS announcements. Around 0740 GMT, the U.S. unit was at\par S$1.7505, down from S$1.7552 late in Asia Wednesday, amid speculation\par the MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb market volatility.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indicating its policy is now back on hold till at least October, the\par MAS said it will continue its "neutral" policy stance and keep the width\par of the re-centered trading band the same.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But data released Thursday called into question the government's\par 0.5%-2.5% full-year growth forecast. And the MAS gave itself more\par flexibility on policy by announcing its next review will be in three\par months, rather than six.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I assume, though they have not said it yet, the government will have\par to lower the full-year forecast," said MMS International's Cohen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With inflation in the first five months of the year at a\par lower-than-expected 0.6% on year, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also cut its\par full-year consumer price index forecast to 0.5%-1% from 0.5%-1.5%\par previously.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                 Lingering SARS Pain\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On an annualized basis, Singapore's GDP shrank 11.8% in the second\par quarter from the January-March period, when it expanded at a 1.2% annual\par rate on a quarter-to-quarter basis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Suan Teck Kin, an economist at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp., said\par that while the second-quarter contraction "is probably the worst we'll\par see," a sharp rebound this quarter isn't in the cards. Suan plans to cut\par his full-year growth forecast by about half a percentage point to "1% or\par less."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Suan expects the services sector, especially hard-hit by SARS, to\par recover only gradually in the third quarter, probably by about 1% on\par year, as sentiment remains weak and the job market soft.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Ministry of Trade and Industry acknowledged that SARS, which\par killed 32 people here, also took an unexpectedly heavy economic toll,\par although the ministry expects the economy to improve in the current six\par months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indeed, the Purchasing Managers' Index, considered a key leading\par indicator of Singapore's manufacturing trends, put in a\par better-than-expected showing in June by marking its first expansion\par after three months of contraction. The PMI is the first set of June\par manufacturing data to be published.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The key manufacturing industry contracted by 7.5% on year in the\par second quarter, while the services industry shrank by 3%, the ministry\par said. The construction sector continued to contract, shrinking by 10.9%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The flash estimates are intended as an early indicator of GDP\par performance in the quarter, and are based largely on data in April and\par May. A more detailed report will be issued in August.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore's weak data cast doubt over Taiwan's official 1.2% on-year\par GDP growth forecast. "I suspect that for Hong Kong and Taiwan, the\par numbers will be worse off," said Joseph Tan, an economist at Standard\par Chartered Bank in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For one thing, SARS was more widespread in those places than Singapore\par and lasted longer. Singapore was taken off the World Health\par Organization's list of SARS-affected regions May 30. Hong Kong, which\par suffered 298 deaths from the illness, got the WHO green light June 23;\par Taiwan, with 84 deaths, was cleared only last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Shen Hong and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150;\par hong.shen@dowjones.com; izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Billy Mallard\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030710dz7a000gf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
