\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Korean won, Taiwan dlr extend gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2003\par 10:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, July 4 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were a mixed\par bag on Friday, although most regionals managed to hold on to\par recent gains, with the South Korean won at a fresh five-month\par high and the Taiwan dollar at a 10-month peak.\par     The won                   hit 1,180.0, its strongest level\par since February 7, aided by foreign portifolio investments that\par have crossed a two year record.\par     However, a dealer in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with some local banks spotted buying U.S.\par dollars, had limited the won's gains.\par  \par     The Taiwan dollar          touched a 10-month high of\par 34.355 as the local stocks index closed at a new one-year high.\par     Elsewhere, dealers said a weaker bias in the Japanese yen\par   and position squaring ahead of the weekend pressured\par regionals downward.\par     But a general lack of trading interest in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par due to a holiday in the United States made players reluctant to\par build aggressive positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Data showing early signs of recovery in Singapore's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par manufacturing sector failed to spark significant activity in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the Singapore dollar , leaving it in a range of 1.7512 to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1.7530 per U.S. dollar, against late Thursday's 1.7511.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index rose 1.4 points to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 50.8 percent in June, after posting three consecutive months of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par readings below 50, which suggested contraction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The improvement is not surprising given that a rebound has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par been expected with key event risks such as the Iraq war and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SARS outbreak out of the way by last month," said Jimmy Koh,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par head of treasury research at UOB Bank in Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still Koh said the rise in some components of the PMI, such\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par as new orders and export orders, suggested higher production\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ahead and confirmed the view that second quarter was Singapore\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economy's lowest point this year and that a recovery was due in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the second half.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data reflecting a less promising exports outlook weighed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par down the Philippine peso  to 53.455 per dollar, against\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 53.39 at close of local trade on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Philippine exports were down nearly four percent from a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par year earlier, testing hopes of stronger economic growth in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par second half of the year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Price pressures also brought bad news, with the inflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rate for the 12 months to June coming in at 3.4 percent, higher\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par than most estimates and the 2.7 percent rise in the year to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said inflationary growth was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unlikely to be sustained in coming months and would not affect\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par its easier monetary policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Wednesday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had delivered an unexpected\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 25 basis point cut in its overnight rates, taking them to their\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lowest level in more than 11 years. It was the first adjustment\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par since March 2002.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The Thai baht        held firm at around 41.65 per dollar,\par just shy of one-week high of 41.63 hit on Thursday.\par     Dealers said equity-related foreign investment inflows and\par dollar sales by exporters had pushed the baht higher.\par     But fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to protect export\par competitiveness and dollar purchases by players covering short\par positions were likely to limit gains.\par     They said several major players had been caught short of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. dollars after the baht declined sharply in response to a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 50 basis point cut in the key 14-day repurchase rate last\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0810 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     118.09       118.10         +0.01\par  Sing dlr      1.7510       1.7511         +0.01\par  Taiwan dlr    34.378       34.447         +0.20\par  Korean won   1180.10      1183.40         +0.28\par  Baht           41.62        41.74         +0.29\par  Peso           53.40        53.39         -0.02\par  Rupiah       8178.00      8210.00         