\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late, Focus On FOMC Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2 1,007 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2003\par 10:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were on a modestly stronger footing late Friday, but off their intraday highs as the dollar clawed back some lost ground. \par \par \uc2  For the most part, market players focused on squaring positions ahead of the weekend, and ahead of the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  The market is "oscillating between a 25-basis-point and a 50-basis-point," rate cut expectation, said a foreign bank trader in Singapore. The dollar bounced off an intraday low of around Y118.01 as speculation once again swung toward a 25-basis-point cut. \par \par \uc2  At 0830 GMT, the dollar was quoted around Y118.32, little changed from Y118.30 overnight in New York, but still down from Y118.60 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  With few local leads, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were largely content to follow the dollar/yen movements. \par \par \uc2  "By and large, dollar/Asians are in a position squaring mode and are looking better-bid on-the-day (as the dollar regained some ground)," said a U.S. bank trader in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Like in the previous session, traders suspected Bank of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the final minutes of trading to push the dollar up after it breached the 1,190-won support, although they said the dollar buying wasn't as heavy as Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A foreign bank trader in Singapore said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to be taking advantage of thin liquidity near the close of trade to maximize the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Liquidity is in the final minutes, so light \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can have a major impact," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at KRW1,190.6, down from KRW1,198.5, but off the intraday low of KRW1,187.0. \par \par \uc2  The won continued to be supported by foreign buying of local shares. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors, who bought Korean shares for the 17th straight day, were selling dollars to settle those purchases, while exporters also unloaded dollar holdings. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Friday, Edaily, an Internet news service, quoted an unidentified official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy as saying that the government will take steps to curb the dollar's sharp fall versus the won, if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "It's not desirable for investors to have excessive expectation of further won strength," the official said, noting such expectation comes from "a speculative mind rather than analysis over economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  He said the government is closely watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "If required, the government may consider using proceeds of regular treasury issues, not only those of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bonds, to stabilize dollar/won," he said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean government can use funds raised from \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bond issues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar weakened after local banks sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session, probably to cover short U.S. dollar positions ahead of the weekend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Talk in the afternoon of a French bank shutting its equity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Singapore may also have given the U.S. dollar a mild leg up, though many dealers said there was no link between the two. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, Societe General SA confirmed that it will close its Singapore equity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid further cost-cutting and efficiency measures. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to connect the two events," said a dealer, "Personally I think the market was just a bit short (U.S. dollars) and they wanted to cover before the weekend," he added. \par \par \uc2  Late in the local session, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7360, up from S$1.7320 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was up for the third straight day as some banks trimmed long-dollar positions on expectations of a U.S. rate cut next week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 53.300 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.350 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The peso also continued to benefit from improved market sentiment on signs the government is finally regaining control of its finances. The government Thursday reported a budget deficit of PHP9.9 billion in May, just half of the PHP20 billion ceiling for the month. \par \par \uc2  But news of a much lower current account surplus for the first quarter pared the peso's gains. \par \par \uc2  The country's current account surplus in the three months to March fell to $338 million from $1.68 billion in the year-ago period due to an unusual rise in imports. \par \par \uc2  "Toward the close, some banks bought back the dollars they sold earlier in the day," said a trader with a foreign bank. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura earlier Friday said he expects the dollar to average PHP53 in 2003. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was up on continued capital inflows, but dollar short covering and corporate demand for the U.S. unit tempered gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed the local session at 8,240 rupiah, down from IDR8,245 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A local newspaper quoted Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah as saying $4 billion of funds has flowed into the country since early April. About half of that was for foreign direct investments, $1 billion was parked in government bonds and mutual funds, and the rest was for speculative dollar-rupiah trades. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar rose as foreign investors continued to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to invest in the local share market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But they said the U.S. dollar was supported by importer purchases, including state-run Chinese Petroleum Corp., which was buying to pay for oil imports. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.593, down from NT$34.614 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan Stock Exchange said overseas investors bought a net NT$4.26 billion worth of local shares Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was little changed as offshore dollar buying in the afternoon trimmed most of its earlier gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed the local session at 41.60 baht, just a tad below Thursday's THB41.615. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Ryan Woo & Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030620dz6k000gc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
