\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Up Late; Off High On Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk \b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2003\par 10:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was stronger late in Asia Monday after some banks unwound their long U.S. dollar positions, but the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pulled off a session high on talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0815 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7294, down from S$1.7338 late Friday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7255 and S$1.7300 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower around S$1.7280 in Singapore after losing ground during Friday's trading session in New York, as market players unwound positions and took profit in the wake of weak U.S. consumer sentiment data. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a session low of S$1.7255 before recovering to around S$1.7280, and subsequently S$1.7300, late in the session, supported by its rebound against the yen. \par \par \uc2  At the end of the Asian trading day, the U.S. dollar was quoted at Y117.58 compared with its earlier low of Y117.16 on speculation Japan's finance ministry may have bought U.S. dollars to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But trade in the local market was generally thin, as market participants were cautious of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore taking similar action to keep the Singapore dollar competitive. \par \par \uc2  Traders are also focused on the MAS' semiannual monetary policy review in early July. \par \par \uc2  While many analysts believe the MAS isn't likely to change its neutral policy bias, they're on the lookout for any hint that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might do otherwise. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary policy tool in seeking to maintain brisk economic growth and low inflation. It guides the Singapore dollar in an undisclosed trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  Until then, most market participants expect thin trade to persist, with major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen setting the tone for the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, the U.S. dollar is expected to stay between S$1.7250 and S$1.7320 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended mostly higher Monday, buoyed by late Friday gains in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the rise in local bond prices was limited as market participants maintained a generally cautious stance ahead of a fresh supply of 10-year government bonds due July 1. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore government is expected to auction between S$2 billion and S$2.5 billion of the bonds later this month, according to dealers. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds slipped four basis points to 0.65%, while the yield on five-year bonds fell one basis point to 1.30%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds fell two basis points to 1.72% and the yield on 10-year bonds slipped two basis points to 1.95%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds ended three basis points lower at 2.37%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swap offers were also mostly softer\par as they tracked local bonds yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The two-year offer was, however, unchanged at 0.85% but the five-year\par offer slipped one basis point to 1.46%. The 10-year offer shed one basis\par point to 2.25%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 140 basis points\par from 141 basis points late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mixed though liquidity remained\par ample in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Monday       Late Friday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.3125% 0.3750%   0.3125% 0.4375%\par 3-Month           0.4375% 0.5625%   0.4375% 0.5625%\par 1-Year            0.5625% 0.6875%   0.5000% 0.6250%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Simon Harvey\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030616dz6g000jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
