\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late; USD Pares Gains Vs Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2003\par 10:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was slightly weaker but off a session low late Thursday in Asia after the U.S. dollar pared early gains against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0815 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7380, up from S$1.7365 late Wednesday in Asia. Intraday, it traded between S$1.7335 and S$1.7410. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened near its session low in Asia, but had risen to around S$1.7385 by the time the Singapore market began trading. It subsequently rose to its intraday high of S$1.7410 around midmorning, after a broader U.S. dollar rise against the yen sparked buying in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  But, gains were limited and the U.S. dollar slipped to S$1.7380-S$1.7390 midday where it stayed for the rest of the Asian session. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise was capped due to the possibility of a further U.S. interest rate cut later this month that could weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, speculation of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also provided support for the U.S. unit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was also faced with a lack of fresh leads to trade off. The next set of key economic data out of Singapore, non-oil domestic exports, aren't due until next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The MAS is also due to make its semiannual monetary policy statement in early July, an event keenly awaited by market participants. \par \par \uc2  Until then, dealers expect the Singapore dollar to continue to take direction from the U.S. dollar's trend versus regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while cautious sentiment keeps trade generally thin, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to trade between S$1.7370 and S$1.7440 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended mixed Thursday, with longer-term bonds mostly a touch lower as they tracked the overnight weakness in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bonds continued to be under selling pressure as market participants positioned for a fresh supply of 10-year government bond issue due July 1. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore government is expected to auction between S$2 billion and S$2.5 billion of new 10-year bonds later this month. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds slipped one basis point to 0.69%, while the yield on five-year bonds held steady at 1.33%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds inched up one basis point to 1.78% and the yield on 10-year bonds rose two basis points to 2.01%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds ended one basis points higher at 2.43%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, swap offers were steady in an equally quiet trading session. \par \par \uc2  The two- and five-year swap offers were both unchanged at 0.86%, and 1.49%, respectively. The 10-year offer was also steady at 2.30%. \par \par \uc2  The spread between two- and 10-year swaps was unchanged at 144 basis points from late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In the local money market, rates were mostly slightly softer as liquidity remained ample in the banking system. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.3750% 0.4375%   0.0625% 0.1875%\par 3-Month           0.4375% 0.5625%   0.5000% 0.6250%\par 1-Year            0.5000% 0.6250%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Simon Harvey & Ryan Woo\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030612dz6c000ij\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
