\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down As USD Makes Late Rebound Vs Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2003\par 12:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was weaker late Tuesday after traders sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the U.S. dollar staged a mild rebound against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 1000 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7328, up from S$1.7285 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7245 and S$1.7330 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened near its session low of S$1.7245 and rose to S$.7280-S$1.7290 in the morning session as it made gains against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained stuck in that range as trading volumes eased, until moving to its intraday high of S$1.7330 midafternoon on renewed buying interest sparked by a general shift higher against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But those gains versus the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were capped by the U.S. unit's retreat against the yen midway through the afternoon session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At the end of Asian trading day, the U.S. dollar was trading at Y118.15 against the yen, after coming off a session high of Y118.35. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they remained wary due to speculation that regional central banks, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions to stem the broader U.S. dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  JPMorgan Chase Bank said in a research note that Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in May suggested "strong U.S. dollar buying by the MAS." \par \par \uc2  The MAS reported over the weekend that reserves were up US$2.44 billion in May from April. \par \par \uc2  "Essentially it backs up anecdotal evidence authorities have continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar weak," to provide additional economic support, JPMorgan said. \par \par \uc2  With the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lingering concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery, dealers said they expect the local dollar to continue to be bound in a tight range against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , broadly tracking its regional counterparts. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the U.S. dollar is expected to stay between S$1.7250 and S$1.7350. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mostly lower Tuesday, with longer-dated bonds closing weaker for the fourth straight session, causing the yield curve to steepen further, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Although there were some concerns over a surprise management shakeup at U.S. government-supported issuer, Freddie Mac, and an inquiry into its accounting practices, local market reaction has been muted so far as attention remained on a fresh supply of 10-year government bonds due July 1. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore government is expected to auction between S$2 billion and S$2.5 billion of the 10-year bonds later this month, and market participants have been actively positioning for the issue in the past few weeks. \par \par \uc2  "If Freddie Mac affects U.S. Treasurys, then Singapore bonds will also follow," though to a lesser extent, said a dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds slipped one basis point to 0.71%, while the yield on five-year bonds inched up two basis points to 1.38%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds rose three basis points to 1.81% and the yield on 10-year bonds rose four basis points to 2.03%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds rose five basis points to 2.45%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swap offers were mixed, with\par longer-dated offers higher as they tracked local bond yields. The\par two-year offer fell one basis point to 0.89%, while the five-year offer\par was steady at 1.53%. The 10-year offer gained four basis points to\par 2.31%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 142 basis points\par from 137 basis points late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady as liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Tuesday      Late Monday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.2500% 0.3750%   0.1875% 0.3125%\par 3-Month           0.5000% 0.6250%   0.5000% 0.6250%\par 1-Year            0.5625% 0.6875%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Simon Harvey\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030610dz6a000mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
