\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn Late;Mkt Wary Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2003\par 11:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave up early gains to move to a weaker footing late Tuesday as market players covered short-dollar positions after selling it down earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Thai baht, Indonesian rupiah and New Taiwan dollar drifted lower. However, the Philippine peso and South Korean won eked out modest gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's recovery against regional units during the session surprised analysts and traders, who had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come under pressure in the wake of the management shake-up at Freddie Mac and weaker U.S. share prices. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be a major factor that helped crimp regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains. The central banks are keen to rein in the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep exports competitive. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar opened lower (versus regional units) and then people took the opportunity to cover short positions. It's a whippy, two-way market," a Singapore-based European bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data seemed to give credence to speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had been supporting the U.S. dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The data, issued over the weekend, showed reserves rose $2.44 billion in May - the largest monthly gain in a couple of years, according to J.P. Morgan Chase Bank. \par \par \uc2  "While some of this reflects EUR (euro) valuation effects and interest accrual, the underlying message is of strong USD (U.S. dollar) buying by the MAS," the bank said in a research note Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "It's definitely making people nervous. That's part of the reason why the dollar/Sing (Singapore unit) is well-bid today," noted Sameer Goel, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Late in the local session, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7318, up from S$1.7285 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted around THB41.80 late in Asia, up from THB41.69 Monday, taking a cue from its rise versus the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Market players were also wary of provoking Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially with memories of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still fresh in their minds. Bank of Thailand bought dollars Friday when the baht spiked to a fresh 10-month high, traders said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears also caused the Indonesian rupiah to reverse early gains. The dollar recovered from an intraday trough of 8,155 rupiah to end the local session at IDR8,210, up from IDR8,195 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said market talk of dollar buying by central banks elsewhere in the region helped push the U.S. unit up versus the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, U.S. dollar demand from government-controlled banks helped weaken the local unit. Several dealers said the dollar buying was likely related to purchases of military equipment. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit ended at NT$34.677, up from NT$34.662 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said government-controlled Farmers Bank of China bought an estimated $100 million, while International Commercial Bank of China was also busy buying U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend, the local language China Times newspaper quoted unidentified Defense Ministry officials as saying Taiwan will sign a deal this month to buy four decommissioned Kidd-class destroyers from the U.S. Navy for US$28.4 billion. \par \par \uc2  Dollar selling by foreign buyers of South Korean shares, and also exporters, pushed the won marginally higher, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a two-and-a-half week closing low of 1,194.5 won, down from KRW1,196.7 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought stocks for a ninth session Tuesday, ending as net buyers of KRW20.2 billion worth of shares. \par \par \uc2  There was also market talk that Daewoo Shipbuilding may soon be unloading some of the $255.6 million it raised last week in a Global Depositary Receipts offering. \par \par \uc2  "We expect they (Daewoo Shipbuilding) will sell at least $230 million," a Seoul-based dealer said. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar isn't expected to slip under KRW1,190 as the government is likely to step in to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended higher as exporters unloaded some of their dollar holdings when the U.S. unit began to gain ground during the session, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at PHP53.365 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.380 Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030610dz6a000m4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
