\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late, Support From Share Buying \b0\par\par\uc2 1,011 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2003\par 11:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned in a mixed performance Monday as they recovered or trimmed early losses on the back of dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  Broadly stronger regional equities' markets provided support to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although upside was capped by concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, Indonesian rupiah and Thai baht were weaker late Monday. The South Korean won, the New Taiwan dollar and the Philippine peso were stronger. \par \par \uc2  A Singapore-based trader at a European bank said the dollar started on a stronger footing against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but lost momentum later in the session. \par \par \uc2  "I thought it (the dollar) was going higher, so I was kind of surprised (when gains weren't sustained). The market started turning around" as participants started selling back dollars, he said. "The market is very short term. Nobody wants to be caught long (dollars)," the trader added. \par \par \uc2  The overall market was quiet, with trading thinned by holidays in Australia and some parts of Europe, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be losing steam after a strong run-up in recent weeks on the back of broad dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  Firming oil prices, weak economic data and a general reluctance by regional central banks to see their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate too fast - and hence erode export competitiveness - may well keep regional units on the defensive. \par \par \uc2  China's pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, which gives it price advantage in the export market, is a "very strong motivating factor" for regional central banks to want to curb the strength of their own units, said David Simmonds, senior emerging markets strategist for Asia at Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  China pegs the value of the yuan to the dollar, allowing it to trade in a very narrow band. \par \par \uc2  With such factors at play, a weak dollar wouldn't necessarily translate into stronger regional units, Simmonds said. However, resilient equity markets combined with a weak dollar would be bullish for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he noted. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won closed at a two-week high on a wave of dollar selling by exporters and foreign investors in the local stock market. Foreigners were net buyers of Korean stocks for an eighth straight session, net buying KRW223.7 billion in shares. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,196.7 won, down from KRW1,200.3 Thursday, marking its strongest close since May 23. South Korean markets were closed for a holiday Friday. \par \par \uc2  Market players who had been expecting the authorities to support the dollar at KRW1,200 were encouraged to test the downside by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's absence from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone expected the BOK (Bank of Korea) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they didn't come," a dealer in Seoul said, adding that the won is expected to continue to trend higher. He projected the dollar's new support at KRW1,190. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar ended modestly higher, helped also by foreign buying of local stocks. Foreign investors bought nearly NT$3.6 billion net in stocks Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.662, slightly down NT$34.665 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The market was very quiet as most players remained on the sidelines after last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Many players who shorted the U.S. dollar Friday were bitten, that is why a lot of them were kind of sluggish today," said a foreign bank trader in Taipei. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market regularly, bought an estimated US$100 million in the final minutes of Friday's session. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso snapped a four-day losing streak and finished higher on the back of remittance inflows and thin demand for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 53.38 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.45 Friday. \par \par \uc2  A Manila-based trader said there was also some profit-taking on the dollar in the afternoon as the market apparently overbought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the session in anticipation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying to boost international reserves. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7285 late in the local session, up from S$1.7235 Friday, but off the intraday peak of S$1.7310. \par \par \uc2  Lingering uncertainties about the local economy weighed on the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Local newspapers cited Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong as predicting flat on-year economic growth in the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  Whether the city-state slips back into recession this year will depend on the fourth quarter, the report cited Goh as saying. \par \par \uc2  Goh said a contraction in the current quarter is a foregone conclusion as the economy was hurt by SARS. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah weakened slightly as the market took a breather amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed local trading at 8,195 rupiah, marginally up from IDR8,180 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Speculative investors have been moving money into Indonesian stocks and bonds in recent months in search of high returns. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has signaled it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect exporters if the dollar falls much further. Traders said fears of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are deterring investors from building up large short-dollar positions. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears also kept the Thai baht on a weaker tone after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, when it rose to a fresh 10-month high. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around 41.68 baht, up from THB41.63 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend, Finance Minister Suchart Jaovisidha said he believed the recent rise in the baht may end within one to two weeks as the global decline in the dollar likely comes to a halt. \par \par \uc2  Suchart said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is closely monitoring the situation. \par \par \uc2  "Let's be frank. If the baht gets very strong (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor) has to do something to make it weaker. He has the mechanisms in his hands," Suchart said. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030609dz69000lq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
