\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Regional Ctrl Bks Buy Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 919 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2003\par 11:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by regional central banks and short covering on the dollar ahead of the weekend reined in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Friday despite the weak tone of the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht and the Philippine peso were lower; the Indonesian rupiah was firmer; and the Singapore dollar and New Taiwan dollar were steady. South Korean markets were closed for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  kicked off the session on a broadly stronger note on the back of a firmer yen; the Thai baht rose to a fresh 10-month high of 41.47 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Singapore said Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via agent banks, aggressively bought dollars from the THB41.50 level through the session. \par \par \uc2  Around early afternoon, Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula told reporters he expects the dollar to regain ground against the baht. He said the baht will soon weaken because it's likely to move in line with regional counterparts. \par \par \uc2  "We are monitoring its movement," he said. At the time of his comments, the dollar was trading around THB41.53. \par \par \uc2  At 0911 GMT, the dollar was quoted around THB41.65, up from THB41.58 at the Asian close late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Last week, Pridiyathorn confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been buying dollars to stem the baht's sharp rise. The baht has consistently chalked up fresh 10-month highs in recent weeks \par \par \uc2  Analysts and traders said the baht is getting a boost from the inflow of foreign capital into the local stock market. Foreign investors have been net buyers of stocks on most days since the beginning of May. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, there was also talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, although several traders said there was little evidence to support the speculation. \par \par \uc2  A European bank trader said several U.S. and local banks were buyers of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that they were likely covering short positions ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Late in the local session, the U.S. unit was quoted at $1.7235, little changed from S$1.7233 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar snapped a four-session winning streak to end flat on heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at its intraday high of NT$34.665, unchanged from Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had traded below NT$34.635 for most of the session amid the inflow of foreign funds into local stocks, and also the strong yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But a flurry of dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late trading pulled up the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the most important pillar for the U.S. dollar today," said a dealer at a foreign bank in Taipei. \par \par \uc2  She estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for about half of Friday's turnover, including nearly US$100 million in the final 15 minutes of trading. Friday's total dealings were valued at US$347 million, compared with US$415 million Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was driven to its lowest closing in more than two months by higher corporate demand for dollars, and also as banks built up dollar positions amid lower domestic interest rates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP53.450 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.330 Thursday, marking its strongest finish since March 31. \par \par \uc2  Banks bought dollars to capitalize on the narrowing of differential between U.S. and domestic rates after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto easing of monetary policy Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reinstated the tiered interest rate system on its overnight borrowing, which effectively lowered the overnight borrowing rate to a three-tiered rate of 1%, 4% and 7%, from a flat 7%. \par \par \uc2  "The restoration of the tiering system makes banks more liquid in pesos, and the dollar is the only attractive instrument to place these funds at this time," said a Manila-based trader with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  Other factors that pressured the peso were the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's revised balance of payments projection, and the planned lifting of the maturity limit on non-deliverable forwards, or NDFs, of banks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier Friday said it expects the balance of payments to swing to a deficit of $1.1 billion this year from last year's $660 million surplus. It was originally expecting surplus of at least $100 million for 2003. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to lift the six-month limit on the maturity of banks' NDFs later this month, announced Thursday, could push up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Banks' clients will be able to spread their exposure between maturities, which could lower the swap rates a bit, but this will have an inverse impact on the exchange rate," said another local bank trader in Manila. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was boosted by continued inflows of funds into the local capital market by investors seeking higher returns, traders said. \par \par \uc2  After a choppy session, the dollar closed local trading at 8,180 rupiah, down from IDR8,225 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Although profit taking pulled the dollar out of its intraday trough of IDR8,170, traders said it remains on track to head lower. \par \par \uc2  "People still believe the dollar will fall to IDR8,100," said a dealer with a local bank. "It's only a matter of time." \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030606dz66000l6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
