\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Late, Pares Early Gains; Tracks Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2003\par 11:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was little changed late Friday as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadily recovered from opening lows, keeping in tune with its movements against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0830 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7235, compared with S$1.7233 late Thursday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7180 and S$1.7235 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened near its session low of S$1.7180, reflecting a broad retreat after weaker-than-expected U.S. jobless claims data and as players shifted funds into the euro after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 50 basis-point interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar staged a modest rebound midway through the morning session, but hovered around S$1.7210-S$1.7220 for rest of the day, before short covering ahead of the weekend pushed it higher late in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's late rise in Singapore also tracked its movements against the yen, which weakened to Y117.99 compared with Y117.30 earlier. \par \par \uc2  The late weakening of the local dollar sparked speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. unit, though many dealers said there was little evidence to support that theory. \par \par \uc2  Concern about the domestic economy's pace of recovery continued to weigh on the local dollar. Some players also noted that the thin market liquidity somewhat exaggerated moves. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to trade sideways later Friday as the weekend nears, keeping to a range of between S$1.7180 and S$1.7250. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mixed Friday, with long bonds mostly lower on pre-weekend profit taking, tracking the overnight retreat in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trade in the local bonds market was, however, thin ahead of a key U.S. employment report due later Friday which could show further signs of weakness in the labor market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds slipped two basis points to 0.70% as the curve continued to steepen while the yield on five-year bonds inched up one basis point to 1.35%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was steady at 1.72% and the yield on 10-year bonds rose two basis points to 1.94%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds rose one basis point to 2.35%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, offers were also mixed as they\par tracked local bond yields. The two-year offer was flat at 0.89%, while\par the five-year offer was also unchanged at 1.48%. The 10-year offer\par gained one basis point to 2.22%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 133 basis points\par from 132 basis points late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady as liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.3750% 0.4375%   0.3750% 0.5000%\par 3-Month           0.5000% 0.6250%   0.5000% 0.6250%\par 1-Year            0.5625% 0.6875%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030606dz66000k6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
