\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late But  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Linger \b0\par\par\uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2003\par 12:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were stronger Friday in the wake of the dollar's overnight weakness against major rivals, especially the yen, which provides broad direction to regional units. \par \par \uc2  Buoyant equity markets also helped support Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But gains were tempered by Japanese government warnings against the yen's rise. Market players also kept a wary eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks in the region. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, central banks in Asia will increasingly deter any trade weighted gain to defend export competitiveness," Bank of America said in a research note Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Asian economies are heavily export-dependent, and need to protect this in an environment of global and U.S. growth concerns," the bank added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players are waiting to see what emerges from the weekend's meeting of the Group of Eight leading nations, particularly for further hints on the U.S.' dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush, in an interview with a group of foreign news organizations Thursday, said he will assure the G-8 meeting that he backs a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Bush's comments came amid growing market perception that the U.S. has retreated from its so-called strong dollar policy, fueled largely by remarks made by Treasury Secretary John Snow earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won closed up at 1,205.2 to the dollar, from KRW1,207.0 Thursday, after trading in a tight band of between KRW1,203.8 and KRW1,206.5. \par \par \uc2  Besides riding on the yen's gains, the won was also bolstered by dollar sales by exporters, and also by foreign investors who have been snapping up local shares for the past three days. \par \par \uc2  But fears that the government might step in to support the dollar below 1,200-won kept the local unit from appreciating further. \par \par \uc2  "People are worried about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a Seoul-based trader said. \par \par \uc2  Against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7318 near the end of the local session, down from S$1.7362 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at NT$34.712, down from Thursday's NT$34.772. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar typically moves in line with the yen owing to export competition between the two economies. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said heavy U.S. dollar sales by Taiwanese exporters to settle accounts ahead of the month end and foreign buying of local shares boosted the local unit. \par \par \uc2  One Taipei-based dealer said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to support the U.S. dollar at around NT$34.70. \par \par \uc2  "Throughout Asia, central banks are supporting the U.S. dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula Friday said the baht has now stabilized following its recent sharp rise to 10-month highs, which prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  "The baht has gained stability," Pridiyathorn told reporters. \par \par \uc2  At 0928 GMT, the dollar was quoted around 41.71 baht, down from THB41.855 Thursday, mirroring the regional trend. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso rebounded from the previous session's two-month lows, propelled by the stronger yen amid thin corporate demand for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP53.240 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.350 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said demand for dollars tapered off as most companies had covered in advance their month-end import and debt payment requirements. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's financial markets were closed for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030530dz5u000n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
