\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up Late; Rupiah At 33-Mo High \b0\par\par\uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2003\par 11:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened Monday, prompting verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by some central banks. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah retreated from its intraday peak but remained at a 33-mohth closing high after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor warned a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hurt exporters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended the local session at 8,175 rupiah, down from IDR8,275 Friday, although off the session low of IDR8,095. Traders said capital inflows into Indonesia and the dollar's broad weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were factors behind the rupiah's rise. \par \par \uc2  The dollar recouped some of its losses after Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah said that "a too rapid rupiah appreciation is not good for business." \par \par \uc2  But he also said the market should set the rupiah's level, indicating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't yet considering significant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The chairman of the Indonesian Textile Producers' Association Benny Sutrisno added that the rupiah's rise is likely to cause problems for local manufacturers who export most of their products. \par \par \uc2  "The rupiah's strength is not competitive and bad for exporters," said Benny. "The costs in terms of dollars will go up, while our export revenues in rupiah terms will fall." \par \par \uc2  Meantime, an appreciating won prompted South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to warn it would actively take measures to support the dollar, resulting in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  giving up all its early gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which earlier fell to a low of KRW1,190.0, finished at KRW1,199.0, up from 1,194.4 Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The government is concerned over the negative impact of an excessive fall in the dollar-won rate and the consequent market jitters on the local economy," Choi Joong Kyung, director general at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy's international finance bureau, said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The government will take "various" steps to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, including issuing won-denominated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bonds, Choi said. \par \par \uc2  There was speculation the authorities began to buy dollars in the market via state-run banks even before the finance ministry statement was released in the afternoon, traders said. \par \par \uc2  For the most part, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to take their cue from the dollar/yen pair, which stayed below Y117.0 in Asia ahead of holidays in U.S. and U.K Monday. \par \par \uc2  At 0921 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y116.88 compared with Y116.85 in New York and Y117.36 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Mostly it's still (a) dollar/yen (story). Support for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming from the yen," said Philip Wee, markets strategist at Singapore's DBS Bank. \par \par \uc2  But he noted that domestic demand in the region has been damped by the outbreak of SARS. \par \par \uc2  "That leaves most countries relying on external factors for growth. Increasingly, if there's no improvement in the external outlook, it's quite hard to see \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in Asia) continuing their appreciating path," Wee added. \par \par \uc2  "We need to see the dollar/yen's weekly closing (at) below Y116 to see much more downside in dollar/Asia," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was buoyed by the broadly weaker U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the market shrugging off weaker-than-expected April industrial output data, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Trading volumes dropped sharply ahead of holidays in the U.S. and U.K. Monday. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of local trading hours, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7212, down from S$1.7228 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's industrial output fell 5% on year in April due to weak output of electronic goods. A Dow Jones Newswires poll had predicted an average 3.2% rise. \par \par \uc2  Dollar selling and equities-related inflows pushed the Thai baht up to a fresh nine-month high. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed the local session at 41.745 baht, down from THB41.91 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's remarks on the baht's level, carried in the Thai language Kaohoon tabloid, helped spur dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper cited Thaksin as saying the baht's level at around THB41 to the dollar is appropriate and not too strong. Thai exports are still competitive despite the stronger baht, he said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investment inflows are also supporting the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the baht's strength is mainly due to the broad dollar weakness, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso firmed on the back of weekend dollar remittance inflows by Filipino working abroad, even as demand for the U.S. unit thinned, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 52.760 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP52.790 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was a tad weaker after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its export competitor South Korea slipped late in the session, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.709, up from NT$34.707 Friday. Trading volume totaled US$327 million, its lowest in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  A Taipei dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure the local dollar remained in line with the won, since Taiwanese firms compete with South Korean companies for export markets. \par \par \uc2  Demand for the local dollar by overseas investors trading Taiwan shares remained brisk, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought a net NT$3.63 billion worth of shares Monday, according to the Taiwan Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030526dz5q000kb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
