\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options-Bets on Sing dlr, won gains as vols rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 355 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2003\par 09:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 13 (Reuters) - Asian options traders are betting on another swift and sharp gain in the Singapore dollar, with implied volatilities rising even as it hit multi-month highs against a globally weak U.S. dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar  has risen 3.4 percent against the dollar in two\par weeks, on the back of a soaring euro       , U.S. economic\par uncertainties and signs that the U.S. authorities do not mind a weaker\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  But as the Sing traded around 1.73 on Monday and Tuesday, a level seen in late January where some traders suspect the central \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its strength, options traders were still betting on further gains. \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility on the one-month Sing dollar option  climbed to 5.15 percent, from 4.5 on Monday and levels below four percent last week. \par \par \uc2  Several risk-reversal deals were reported, involving dual option contracts. One-month risk reversals were quoted at 0.25 on Tuesday, favouring Sing dollar call options \par \par \uc2  "People are buying downside (on dollar/Sing) and selling topside," an options trader said. \par \par \uc2  "They are buying U.S. dollar puts and Sing dollar calls, and financing them by selling U.S. dollar calls and Sing dollar puts," he added. \par \par \uc2  Under those deals, holders stood to make huge profits if the Sing dollar rose further and big losses if it fell fast, but see little impact if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied at current levels. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals measure the premium investors are prepared to pay to buy call options over put options. \par \par \uc2  In the Korean market, players were clearly bullish on the won, which was trading around 1,200 per dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The bullishness saw traders buying won call, dollar put options, even after the Bank of Korea cut its overnight call rate to 4.00 percent - matching its record low - on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The won has stabilised after rising to a one-month high near 1,189 last week. Implied one-month won volatility was also steady at 8.75 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030513dz5d00flc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
