\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late; Yen Casts Shadow On Region \b0\par\par\uc2 836 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2003\par 11:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled Friday, caught in the downdraft of a falling yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell against both the dollar and the euro, prompting speculation that the Japanese government was covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro climbed as high as Y135.26 in late afternoon trade, its strongest performance against the yen since the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debuted in January 1999. The euro also drew support from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision Thursday to stand pat on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The dollar remained comfortably above the Y117 level for the entire session, adding to talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was keeping a floor under the dollar. Japanese authorities believe a weaker yen will stimulate export growth and help the country escape its long-running economic malaise. \par \par \uc2  The yen's tumble pressured other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, except for the rupiah, which continued to lure investors attracted by relatively higher returns in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the dollar's sharp recovery to a high of Y117.50 Friday from a low of near Y116 Thursday bore the fingerprints of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We have been expecting for some time that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resume ...," UBS Warburg said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumors were fueled by comments from Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that he believes the yen is too strong against the dollar. Shiokawa declined to comment on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar lost ground after climbing to a two-month high against the U.S. dollar Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7420, up from S$1.7372 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar briefly dropped below S$1.7400 in the morning session, before bouncing back in line with its gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Despite the decline in the Singapore dollar, dealers said sentiment toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained positive in light of the city-state's progress in the fight against severe acute respiratory syndrome. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also tracked the yen lower in thin trading. The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.775, up from NT$34.719 Thursday. Trading volume fell to US$257 million from US$402 million Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Importers and exporters were mostly on the sidelines Friday, looking for clear signs as to the near-term direction for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Taiwanese exporters hold large amounts of U.S. dollar's which could limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside in coming sessions. However, continuing reports of new SARS cases in Taiwan have damped sentiment toward the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some dealers in Seoul said the dollar's rise against the won may have been partly the result of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korean monetary authorities aiming to cap the local unit's recent strength. \par \par \uc2  The won's appreciation of nearly 5% against the dollar over the past month has caused South Korean authorities to worry that the country's export competitiveness could suffer. \par \par \uc2  "The government made it clear that it doesn't want the dollar to fall further," a dealer at KorAm Bank said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,200.4 won, up from Wednesday's close of KRW1,199.0. The Seoul \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was closed Thursday due to holiday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah continued to hover near a 21-month high against the dollar as investors remained bullish about economic prospects in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at IDR8,545 in late Asian trading, down from IDR8,555 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The sale of the 51% Bank Danamon stake to a consortium comprising Singapore's Temasek Holdings and Deustche Bank has helped boost market confidence that the government is serious in implementing economic reform programs. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also cut short-term interest rates Friday for the fourth time this year in a move that is expected to spur economic growth, and help relieve the bad-debt problem in the country. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened in line with its regional counterparts. Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at 42.545 baht, up from THB42.450 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  However, the Thai baht is expected to draw support from the country's strong export performance going forward. \par \par \uc2  Commerce Minister Adisai Bodharamik said Friday he expects April exports to grow 15% to 20% from a year earlier, based on strong preliminary data from the customs department. Customs-based exports were up 16% on year in March. \par \par \uc2  Against the peso, the dollar closed at PHP52.340, up from PHP52.150 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura Friday said he was satisfied with the peso's current level against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Over the past two weeks, remittance inflows from overseas Filipino workers, sending their pay home for tuition fees ahead of the start of a new school term, have fueled a rally in the peso recently. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4045; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030509dz59000ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
