\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed, Singapore Dlr Still Pressured \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2003\par 12:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Wednesday at the tail end of a lack luster trading session during which the Singapore dollar's malaise continued as worries over the economic effects of the deadly SARS outbreak lingered, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over SARS also loomed large in Hong Kong and China, the two countries where SARS has claimed the most victims. The premium on one year Hong Kong dollar forwards remained at five-month highs despite a HK$11.8 billion government relief package to cushion the economic impact of the SARS outbreak. \par \par \uc2  The one-year forward was quoted at a premium of 305-315 to spot late Wednesday, up from 290-300 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the discount on the benchmark one-year non-deliverable forward rate on the Chinese yuan continued to narrow, and was quoted around 105/65 points to the spot rate late Wednesday, from 145/85 late Tuesday, and 350/330 Monday. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, the other main SARS-hit country in Asia, concerns about the health of the economy continued to damp market sentiment, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7770, up from S$1.7758 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, the government said visitor arrivals slumped 68% on year to 48,600 for the week of April 8-14 as tourists continued to stay away to avoid contracting SARS. So far, the outbreak has killed 16 people and infected around 178 in the city state. \par \par \uc2  Still, any further weakness in the Singapore dollar may be limited in the near term by the widely-held view that it is currently trading near the limits of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore last week reaffirmed its neutral monetary policy, in effect putting a cap to further \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar selling as players worried about triggering \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Among other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Philippine peso was also modestly weaker, while the Indonesian rupiah, South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar were marginally stronger. The Thai baht was little changed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP53.510 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP52.495 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Apart from follow-through dollar buying by companies for month-end requirements, worries over the government's continuing budget deficit overshoot and the economic impact of SARS, also dominated the market. \par \par \uc2  "The peso's fall, however, was capped on speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to provide dollar liquidity in defense of the local unit," a trader said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials couldn't be immediately reached. \par \par \uc2  As against the South Korean won, the dollar closed at KRW1,218.9, down from KRW1,219.4 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Market players took profits following the dollar's sharp gain in the previous session, while exporters sold above KRW1,220, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be a consensus in the market that the dollar's 10-won surge yesterday (Tuesday) was overdone," said a trader at a major South Korean bank. \par \par \uc2  Trading wasn't very active as players monitored developments in Beijing where the U.S., North Korea and China are holding talks on Pyongyang's suspected nuclear arms program. The talks are scheduled to conclude Friday. \par \par \uc2  "People aren't expecting big results from the talks and prospects for the Beijing meeting aren't at all rosy," said another trader at a foreign financial institution. \par \par \uc2  Selling by Taiwan exporters and offshore players pulled the U.S. unit down a tad to end at 34.812 versus the local dollar, from NT$34.816 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Bids for U.S. dollars from local insurers and importers at the NT$34.80 level capped the local unit's gains, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah resumed its upward march after a brief breather in the previous session following six days of gains. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended Wednesday at IDR8,730, down from IDR8,740 Tuesday. Analysts attribute the rupiah's upward momentum to investors' search for higher yields. \par \par \uc2  As against the Thai baht, the dollar ended the local session at THB42.755, quite steady compared with THB42.760 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Barry Deely \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030423dz4n000or\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
