\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late; Coalition War Advance Lifts Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2003\par 11:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Broad dollar strength fueled by the progress of coalition military forces in Iraq kept most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the defensive Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, under additional pressure due to concerns over the spread of a lethal strain of pneumonia, fell to a five-month low against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Reports that soldiers from the U.S. 3rd Infantry Division were in the process of securing control of Baghdad's main airport boosted the dollar against its major rivals, as well as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, a further weak U.S. economic data and uncertainty about how the "battle of Baghdad" may play out tempered the enthusiasm for the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The Institute for Supply Management reported Thursday that U.S. service-sector activity contracted in March due to war concerns, ending 13 straight months of expansion. The Labor Department, meanwhile, said claims for unemployment insurance rose to their highest level in 11 months. \par \par \uc2  "We see a sluggish (U.S.) postwar recovery as the main risk to any forecast - although likely a limited one, since interest rate cuts would follow," Bank of America said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  For Asian economies, however, a more immediate concern is the impact of severe acute respiratory syndrome, a deadly virus that has claimed 73 lives in the region and infected hundreds more. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has been particularly hard hit by the negative sentiment fueled by SARS. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7790, up from S$1.7715 late Thursday. The U.S. dollar had hit a five-month high of S$1.7800 in overnight trading. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were emboldened to sell the Singapore dollar after it weakened beyond a level at which some analysts thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This was interpreted by some traders as an implicit indication of the MAS's willingness to allow a weaker Singapore dollar to act as a cushion against a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean won, the dollar edged up to KRW1,258.0 at Friday's close from KRW1,255.0 a day earlier in light trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains were limited by anticipated exporter dollar sales above the KRW1,260/dollar level and rumors of possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Importers, meanwhile, were believed to be looking at the U.S. unit falling to KRW1,255 before stepping in to buy the greenback, players said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the U.S. dollar gain against the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.825, up from Thursday's NT$34.812. \par \par \uc2  The official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled the U.S. dollar off its intraday low of NT$34.801 late in the session. \par \par \uc2  Taiwanese authorities routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they believe it is trading out of line with its regional peers. Weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as a way of preserving the country's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was pressured by dollar demand from oil importers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at its intraday high of 53.250 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.110 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said oil companies stepped up their dollar purchases to cover their import requirements ahead of a long weekend. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine financial markets are closed Monday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah strengthened slightly as fears of violent anti-war protests in the country continued to subside. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 8,885 rupiah in late trading, down from IDR8,990 late Thursday. The dollar had traded as high as IDR8,910 during the session. \par \par \uc2  Market participants have been concerned that the U.S.-led military attacks on Iraq would spark a fierce anti-west backlash in Indonesia, the world's most populous Muslim country. But, so far anti-war protests remain largely peaceful. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar was quoted at THB43.090 near the end of Asian trading, up from THB42.980 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The pair traded in a fairly narrow range of THB43.050 to THB43.140 during Friday's session. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey and Kevin Lim \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030404dz44000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
