\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; SARS, War News Boost Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2003\par 12:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the dollar Thursday, pressured by a combination of dollar-positive Iraq war news and concerns that a deadly pneumonia virus could derail regional economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The advance of U.S. troops to within miles of Baghdad, with relatively few casualties, provided a lift for the dollar as hopes were renewed that the war could be relatively short-lived. \par \par \uc2  However, some market participants expressed concern that once inside the Iraqi capital, coalition forces might get bogged down in bloody street fighting that could include the use of chemical or biological weapons by Iraqi troops. \par \par \uc2  "It is still not clear how the war will turn out," DBS Bank said in a research note. "The significant push by U.S.-led forces toward Baghdad over the past 24 hours does provide some hope that the war may end soon." \par \par \uc2  The parade of investment banks cutting their economic growth forecasts for Asia due to the spread of severe acute respiratory syndrome continued Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ING Financial Markets was the latest to weigh in, trimming its 2003 growth forecast for Hong to 2.5% from 3%. Over the past few days more than half-a-dozen banks and brokerages have cut their forecasts for various Asian economies, citing the negative impact SARS is having on the tourism, retail and consumer services industries. \par \par \uc2  Asia has been the epicenter of the SARS outbreak, accounting for more than 90% of the 2,223 cases reported worldwide. In addition, 72 of the 78 SARS deaths globally have occurred in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has been hit particularly hard by the SARS outbreak. Singapore ranks third behind China and Hong Kong in reported SARS cases, although officials said there are signs the situation is stabilizing in the city-state. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7715, up from S$1.7690 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise was kept in check by concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a trade-weighted band that the MAS uses as a guideline for managing the Singapore dollar's movements. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. dollar closed at its intraday high of NT$34.812, up from Wednesday's close of NT$34.798. \par \par \uc2  Exporters helped slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall by selling U.S. dollars above the NT$34.80 level, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won gave up its early gains to finish marginally weaker. Foreigners' sales of local stocks weighed on the won late in the session. \par \par \uc2  Foreigners sold a net 58.6 billion won in shares Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at KRW1,255.0, slightly up from Wednesday's close of KRW1,254.0. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen and a report from local online news provider MoneyToday underpinned the won early in the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The report quoted Moody's senior analyst Thomas Bryne as saying the credit outlook for South Korea is a bit better, with U.S.-South Korea ties growing more solid. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso recouped its early losses to finish slightly higher in quiet trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at its intraday low of PHP53.110, down from PHP53.160 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to its session high of PHP53.360 during morning trading as news of a deadly bombing in the southern city of Davao overnight fueled peso selling. \par \par \uc2  The dollar lost ground later in the day as demand for peso-denominated assets picked up and security concerns faded. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah weakened slightly as dollar demand from local importers perked up. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,890 rupiah, up slightly from Tuesday's close of IDR8,885. Financial markets in Jakarta were closed for a public holiday Wednesday and offshore rupiah trading is virtually nonexistent. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht tracked the yen's late session decline to finish Asian hours slightly weaker. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at 42.98 baht, up from THB42.96 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Jitters over SARS curtailed trading volume by keeping many players out of the market. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Simon Harvey \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030403dz43000pw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
