\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late On Continued SARS Fears \b0\par\par\uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2003\par 11:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar continued to be hit by worries over the effects of SARS on the local economy Thursday, though its fall was limited by concerns that it may have dropped below its trade-weighted policy band. \par \par \uc2  At 0815 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7715, up from S$1.7690 late in Asia Wednesday. It traded between S$1.7698 and S$1.7720 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.7700, following overnight gains against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on hopes the U.S.-led war in Iraq could end swiftly. \par \par \uc2  Sporadic U.S. dollar buying continued through the session on concerns the domestic economy would see slower growth as a result of the spread of severe acute respiratory syndrome, or SARS, and its impact on the local retail and tourism industries. \par \par \uc2  Keeping a cap on the U.S. dollar was the fear that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have fallen outside its trade-weighted policy band, a development that might invite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Standard Chartered Bank said the MAS may have already allowed the Singapore dollar to slip outside the policy band to accommodate poor economic visibility. \par \par \uc2  United Overseas Bank advised market participants to watch what it estimated to be the limits of the MAS band at S$1.7740-50 for signs of implicit easing. \par \par \uc2  "A decisive break of this level will be interpreted by the market as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opening up its gates," UOB said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, dealers don't expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to break above the S$1.7750 mark yet, especially with the weekend nearing, while support is expected at S$1.7670. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mostly higher in thin trade late Thursday as market attention remained fixed on developments in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds was down one basis point at 0.91%, while the yield on five-year bonds was down two basis points at 1.54%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was one basis point lower at 1.94%, while the yield on 10-year bonds slipped two basis points to 2.17%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was steady at 2.58%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, offers were mostly lower as they\par tracked local bond yields. The two-year offer fell two basis points to\par 1.04%, while the five-year swap offer slipped one basis point to 1.65%.\par The 10-year offer was, however, up five basis points at 2.37%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 133 basis points\par from 126 basis points late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady as liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late    Thursday  Late    Wednesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.6250% 0.6875%   0.7500% 0.8125%\par 3-Month           0.6250% 0.7500%   0.6250% 0.7500%\par 1-Year            0.6875% 0.8125%   0.6875% 0.8125%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4150;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030403dz43000m5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
