\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: SARS, Weak Econ To Drive Singapore Dollar South \b0\par\par\uc2  By Izham Ahmad \par \uc2 1,008 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2003\par 09:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- A cold wind has been blowing from the north,\par giving much of Asia - and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - the chills.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The mysterious flu-like disease - known as the severe respiratory syndrome or SARS because the virus that causes it has yet to be identified - has killed at least 62 and infected over 1,600 across three continents. Most of the victims were in mainland China, where the disease is believed to have originated, and Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, one of the worst affected places in the region, tourism has borne the brunt of the health scare, and with it the Singapore dollar. Four people in Singapore have died and over 90 have the disease. \par \par \uc2  Since the first cases were reported in early March, the U.S. dollar has risen to S$1.7740 in intraday trading - the highest level this year. At 0655 GMT Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was changing hands at S$1.7665. \par \par \uc2  The scare couldn't have come at a worse time. The Singapore economy was already poised to turn in a weaker performance, taking the impact of the Iraq war into consideration. \par \par \uc2  Now, with the SARS virus on the loose, the economy could weaken even further. Over the weekend, Deputy Prime Minister and Finance Minister Lee Hsien Loong said gross domestic product could expand at the lower end of the government's forecast 2%-to-5% this year. Some private-sector economists have slashed their gross domestic product forecasts to a range of between 2.0% and 3.5%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    NODX Up, Production Index Falls In Feb\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par G.K. Goh Research, for instance, now expects GDP to expand 2% from\par 3.8% previously, and for GDP in the second quarter to grow 1.7% from 3%\par previously. The firm's forecasts assume the health problem will stretch\par another six to seven months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's the combined effect of everything, the impact of SARS, and the\par risk of the war going into the third quarter, and the\par weaker-than-expected manufacturing numbers," Song Seng Wun, G.K. Goh's\par regional economist, said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Recent economic data have begun showing the impact of the global\par uncertainties. The important manufacturing sector, for instance, has\par shown a conflicting performance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Non-oil domestic exports for February rose a nominal 23% over the year\par to S$7.75 billion, stronger than January's 18.3% increase, as sales of\par both electronic and non-electronic products remained strong. However,\par the more forward-looking industrial output index slipped 1.4% over the\par year for the same month. The index's fall spooked the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par which had expected a rise of 12.5%. The news sent the Singapore dollar\par down about 15 points to S$1.7682 against the U.S. dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, analysts are also starting to assess the potential damage\par on the economy from the SARS-related concerns. One industry they are\par looking at closely is tourism, and the knock-on effects on the commerce\par and retail sectors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The stock market has already started to reflect some of these\par concerns. Share prices of several hotel operators, airlines and retail\par firms with both local and regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have already fallen as\par investors expect these firms to be hard hit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tourism, which accounts for about 10% of GDP, depends heavily on\par visitors from regional countries, the number one market being Indonesia.\par Last week, the Indonesian government issued a statement warning its\par citizens not to travel to countries affected by SARS, including\par Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par G.K. Goh's Song said the impact on tourism could last right through to\par the end of the year, especially taking into account the prospects of a\par more prolonged war.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore Tourism Board told Dow Jones Newswires exclusively last\par week that preliminary indicators showed that 440,682 visitors arrived in\par Singapore in the March 1-23 period, 8.8% lower than a year ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Tourism Board's 2003 Forecasts To Stay\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Official data for February have yet to be released, but for January,\par tourism arrivals were up 7.4% over the year, with the increase coming\par mostly from the China market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For the full year, the tourism board had forecast growth of 1%-to-3%\par for both visitor arrivals and revenues from tourism-related services.\par The board said that it remained confident of that forecast as long as\par the war doesn't drag and the global economy isn't "severely affected."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Domestically, the economic impact of the SARS outbreak on top of the\par hesitant economic numbers, will limit any undue SGD strength," Bank of\par Tokyo-Mitsubishi said in a research report Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's response to the SARS scare has so far been\par orderly, which means that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par unlikely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in any way. It is also unlikely to change its\par policy stance, which currently is in neutral. This means that the MAS\par lets the Singapore dollar trade within an undisclosed band against a\par basket of its trade partners' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the economy weakens further, there is a likelihood that the\par authority could loosen policy further to help exports, economists said.\par In that situation, the Singapore dollar can be expected to soften even\par more.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the meantime, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is expected to test the upper limit\par of the MAS's preferred range. Jimmy Koh, head of treasury research at\par United Overseas Bank Ltd., who believes the upper limit is between\par S$1.7800 and S$1.7815, said that, on a trade-weighted basis, the\par Singapore dollar is currently at its weakest point since January 2002.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi economist Emmanuel Ng, also said the\par trade-weighted Singapore dollar currently stands in the weaker half of\par the policy band, and believes the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair\par could test his estimated limit of S$1.7780-S$1.7800.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par    -Edited by Lim Mui Khi\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030401dz41000i4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
