\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Late; Off Early High Vs US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 09:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was steady late Monday after selling by local banks pulled it off its morning high against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and amid broader U.S. dollar weakness, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0830 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7360, flat with S$1.7357 late Friday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7340 and S$1.7365 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.7345 in the local market and briefly touched a session low of S$1.7340, before bouncing back up to around S$1.7340-S$1.7350 midmorning as it strengthened versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar inched up to its session high after the midday break, allegedly lifted by modest bids by local banks, despite the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  having gained little ground against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen. \par \par \uc2  "I expected (U.S. dollar) support to be around the late Friday low of S$1.7325, but we didn't even reach there, before the (U.S.) dollar bounced," an Asian bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  According to traders, the buying may have been on behalf of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as concerns of similar \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by other regional central banks were circulating in the market. \par \par \uc2  The MAS currently retains a neutral exchange rate-driven monetary policy stance, though it's believed to prefer only a limited strengthening in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar in the interest of maintaining the republic's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers said continued fears over the prospects of a U.S.-led war with Iraq, and its implications for the global economy, will continue to be the focus of regional financial markets. The U.S. dollar will be under pressure as a result. \par \par \uc2  "All the talk that war may be imminent is expectedly keeping the (U.S.) dollar heavy on the topside here in Asia," the Asian bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, dealers expect the U.S. dollar to continue to face selling pressure and support at S$1.7325 isn't likely to hold in the near-term. Next support lies at the psychologically important S$1.7300 mark. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to meet resistance at S$1.7380. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were higher late Monday, as they tracked the late Friday rally in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local bond yields tumbled yet again to fresh historic lows in brisk buying activity, as investors, concerned about the prospects of a war, continued to seek refuge in safe-haven instruments, especially longer-term bonds. \par \par \uc2  At the end of the session, the yield on two-year bonds was five basis points lower at 0.77%, while the yield on five-year bonds was eight basis points lower at 1.25%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yields on seven- and five-year bonds were both 14 basis points lower each at 1.72% and 1.96%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds was 15 basis points lower at 2.34%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, offers were also lower, as they\par tracked local bond yields. The two-year offer fell seven basis points to\par 0.93%, while the five-year swap offer fell 11 basis points to 1.46%. The\par 10-year offer was 13 basis points lower at 2.12%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 119 basis points\par from 126 basis points late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady near their recent\par lows as liquidity remained flush in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Monday       Late Friday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.5000% 0.6250%   0.4375% 0.5000%\par 3-Month           0.5625% 0.6875%   0.5625% 0.6875%\par 1-Year            0.6250% 0.7500%   0.6250% 0.7500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030310dz3a000k2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
