\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up;Singapore $ Ignores Econ Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 10:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- A firmer yen guided most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher late Thursday amid generally muted reaction to local economic data and other news. \par \par \uc2  But trading ranges were narrow as suspicion of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities put a floor under the dollar-yen rate. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen has been the main factor in the market. The rest of the dollar/Asians are being weighed down," by the U.S. unit's weakness against the yen, said a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "Generally, the dollar looks a bit heavy," against regional units, said a trader at another foreign bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit briefly breached the Y117.00 psychological support for the first time since Sept. 3. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated the ministry is watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely and is "always ready" to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed. \par \par \uc2  He declined to comment on whether the MOF has been carrying out covert \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. \par \par \uc2  There was little sign of other Asian monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the dollar's slide against their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though some traders think the Bank of Thailand may have bought the U.S. unit when it neared the 42.65 baht level earlier in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted around THB42.58, down from THB42.75 at the close of the local session Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  United Overseas Bank in a report also noted some talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore may have been in the market, though it was skeptical of those rumors. \par \par \uc2  The bank said the local dollar was not anywhere near the stronger end of the MAS's current policy band - which it estimates to be between $1.7300-$1.7450 to the U.S. unit - so there was no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late in the local session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was quoted around S$1.7349 per U.S. unit, from S$1.7344 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Market players largely ignored the Ministry of Trade and Industry's detailed assessment of the city state's economic performance for last year. \par \par \uc2  The ministry confirmed the earlier 2.2% GDP growth estimate for 2002, despite a revision of fourth-quarter growth to 3% from the previously estimated 2.6%. It also issued a mixed outlook for the domestic economy amid continuing uncertainty over a possible U.S.-led war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The data came a day before Singapore's budget statement for the next fiscal year starting April 1. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar rose as exporters sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay salaries and other monthly expenses on the last trading day for the month, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's gains were also a boost for the local dollar, which closed at its intraday high of NT$34.746 to the U.S. unit, strengthening from Wednesday's NT$34.784. \par \par \uc2  Traders suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the afternoon session, but this was offset by U.S. dollar selling by exporters. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's financial markets will be closed Friday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean won, the dollar ended at KRW1,187.0, slightly down from KRW1,188.5 in the previous session. The intraday range was a narrow KRW1,185.1 to KRW1,189.0. \par \par \uc2  Regular end-of-month dollar selling by exporters was "less than expected this month" and mostly offset by importers' buying, a dealer in South Korea said. \par \par \uc2  There was little reaction to the naming of Kim Jin-Pyo as South Korea's new finance minister. A trader said the appointment wasn't a surprise. \par \par \uc2  Generally, market sentiment is toward buying dollars on dips "considering lingering geopolitical concerns about North Korea and worries about the current account deficit (in Korea) and rising oil prices," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar closed at the intraday high of 54.375 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.350 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A foreign bank trader said there was dollar inflow from Filipinos working overseas, but this failed to aid the peso as they were snapped up by corporates and banks building up their holdings of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian rupiah, the dollar ended at 8,895 rupiah, from IDR8,890 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped any further rises in the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; (603)-2692-5254; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Ryan Woo \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030227dz2r000m5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
