\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Down as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears resurface. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan \par \uc2 822 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2003\par 10:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Feb 26 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  erased part of their offshore gains on Wednesday, following the yen down as worries over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the sensitivity of regional central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength resurfaced. \par \par \uc2  North Korea's missile test on Tuesday, after a series of claims about its nuclear intentions, remained a factor for the Korean won but seemed unimportant to other regional markets. \par \par \uc2  Apart from the yen, local growth concerns amid the risk of a Gulf war still influenced trade, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Thai Baht and the Korean won struck multi-week highs offshore as the yen reached its strongest in nearly six-months. \par \par \uc2  But those gains were pared after market sources said Japan had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen on Tuesday, and Japanese officials warned they were monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  That put a brake on the yen's bull run, spurred by the nomination of Toshihiko Fukui as Bank of Japan governor and encouraged by Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's comment that he had agreed with his Group of Seven counterparts to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to market forces. \par \par \uc2  "North Korea seems to be a risk, predominantly for Korea. But, even there, the main risk is the unknown of how a war in Iraq will affect global trade and how long it will keep oil prices high, which depresses global growth," said Ray Farris, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "You have dollar-yen going lower but most of the governments in the region reluctant to see their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate in line with the yen, so a lot of these governments are effectively engineering weaker exchange rates on a trade-weighted basis," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar settled around 1.7340 after failing to hold on to a three-week high of 1.7320 per U.S. dollar. Analysts said the Sing dollar, up 1.7 percent in two weeks, was nearing levels beyond which its appreciation was difficult. \par \par \uc2  Singapore had another encouraging data release on Wednesday, with industrial production expanding 13.6 percent on the year in January. Gross domestic product figures for 2002 are due on Thursday and the annual budget will be released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Significant Sing dollar upside is unlikely to stretch for now. For one, market is watching the critical 1.73 level, at which MAS (\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore) presence was previously cited," said a commentary from United Overseas Bank. \par \par \uc2  Also, there would be U.S. dollar short-covering if an invasion of Iraq was launched, UOB said. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar came off a two-week high as the yen slid, while the Indonesian rupiah maintained a listless range just above 8,900. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened to 42.74/78 towards close of Asian trading, moving slowly away from an offshore three-week high quote of 42.60. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won ended onshore trade weaker at 1,188.5. The yen-won cross rate was 10.115, showing the worries over North Korea were still weighing on the won, putting it in a relatively weaker position versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  PESO WEAK AGAIN \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso slipped once again to 54.40 but markets, wary of more verbal or physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were assured by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it had not defended the peso on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Philippine markets were closed on Tuesday for a holiday. The peso had hit a two-year low of 54.48 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The announcement of the January budget deficit in the afternoon also surprised markets, both because it came a day early and at 13.95 billion pesos was below the target of an average monthly deficit of 16.8 billion pesos in 2003. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at 54.35, but markets were braced for persistent pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on account of high global oil prices and worries about sanctions if the Philippines does not pass an anti-money-laundering law. \par \par \uc2  The Paris-based Financial Action Task Force has given the country until March 15 to strengthen its law or face sanctions. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0830 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.40       117.33         -0.06\par   Sing dlr      1.7348       1.7330         -0.10\par   Taiwan dlr    34.784       34.740         -0.13\par   Korean won   1188.50      1185.10         -0.29\par   Baht           42.75        42.68         -0.16\par   Peso           54.35        54.37         +0.04\par   Rupiah       8888.00      8890.00         +0.02\par   Change on year\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.40       118.74         +1.14\par   Sing dlr      1.7348       1.7341         -0.04\par   Taiwan dlr    34.784       34.753         -0.09\par   Korean won   1188.50      1185.80