\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Find a footing, but vulnerable. \b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2003\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 13 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  settled in narrow bands against the U.S. dollar after early some choppiness on Thursday, but analysts said there was high risk of them falling sharply if the tensions over Iraq or North Korea rose. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and the Korean won were hemmed in after having rallied from two-month lows this week, while the Thai baht and the Taiwan dollar have been rising after revisiting their end-2002 lows this week. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar see-sawed against the yen in Asia, pressured by worries over the Iraq stand-off and rumours the United States might again raise its security alert level, while the yen benefitted from Japanese exporter sales and fund repatriation. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to hold their ground until Friday, when U.N. weapons inspectors report on Iraq's compliance with a directive to disarm. \par \par \uc2  "From now until Friday, Asian regionals will be hovering in a range. But the bias will be weak on account of geopolitical risks, the yen, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the regional central banks," said Jimmy Koh, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at United Overseas Bank. \par \par \uc2  Koh said the Korean won would be most susceptible. The won weakened at the open after North Korea was declared in breach of the United Nation's atomic safeguards and the matter was referred to the U.N. Security Council, but it then headed back towards Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, this is the next flash point to watch for - North Korea had said that if further sanctions are imposed, that would be tantamount to a declaration of war - but we take the view that the actual military confrontation is extremely unlikely," JP Morgan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist James Malcolm said in a note. \par \par \uc2  SING DOLLAR, BAHT RECOVER \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened as far as 1.7592 as the yen softened towards 121.50 per dollar, and then rallied. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still quite expensive in trade-weighted terms, implying it had substantial room to move lower. They also said trade in regional crosses, involving short Sing dollar positions against the won or Thai baht, was keeping it on the defensive. \par \par \uc2  The baht found support around 43 per dollar, and on Thursday was the only regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be stronger than its 2002 close against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  It touched a six-week low of 43.20 on Tuesday, but analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will use any regional downtrend to buy dollars as the early reprepayments of IMF loans begin. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Thailand will repay $950 million of an IMF-arranged loan, leaving $3.85 billion outstanding. The government intends to clear the loans by July. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, analysts were mixed about the impact of a decision by the influential California Public Employees' Retirement System (CalPERS) to review investments in the region. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, a CalPERS consultant recommended the fund put Thailand and Malaysia back on its list of approved investments and drop the Philippines. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the amounts the fund had previously invested in these countries was quite low, and the impact, if any, would be on sentiment rather than flows into these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  PESO STILL VULNERABLE \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso slipped slightly to end at 53.95, raising concern it may head back to last week's 18-month low of 54.10 per dollar, from where it was pulled away by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warnings on dollar hoarding and some suspsected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It got some brief respite from selling after reports that the January budget deficit was around 15 billion pesos, under the government target of 16.8 billion for the month. \par \par \uc2  But the country's budget problems and worries about a sovereign ratings downgrade, combined with its exposure to global oil prices, placed the peso under considerable stress, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was volatile, moving close to Tuesday's six-week lows of 8,980 per dollar and then rallying to 8,938. Traders said there was no \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Indonesian markets, along with those in Malaysia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  were closed on Wednesday for a public holiday. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0840 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen     120.83       120.76         -0.06\par   Sing dlr      1.7547       1.7532         -0.09\par   Taiwan dlr    34.817       34.815         -0.01\par   Korean won   1202.30      1199.10         -0.27\par   Baht           43.01        43.03         +0.05\par   Peso           53.95        53.93         -0.04\par   Rupiah       8942.00      8973.00         +0.35\par   Change on year\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     120.83       118.74         -1.73\par   Sing dlr      1.7547       1.7341         -1.17\par   Taiwan dlr    34.817       