\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Down Late; Peso Continues Recovery \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2003\par 10:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The threat of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the embattled Philippine peso to continue its recovery Monday, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Philippine President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo over the weekend reiterated the government's pledge to defend the peso after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an 18-month low versus the dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  "We will do all within our power to keep the rate stable, and I ask the cooperation of the financial sector in this effort," Arroyo said in her weekly radio broadcast. \par \par \uc2  The peso breached the key psychological level of 54 to the dollar Friday as concerns about the impact of a possible war in Iraq cast a shadow on the economy in the Philippines and elsewhere in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The threat of war in Iraq, which has provided enormous downward pressure on the dollar so far this year, is now being viewed by market players as negative for Asia as well, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the aggressive dollar selling in recent weeks appears to have subsided in advance of this Friday's U.N. weapons inspectors' report to the U.N. Security Council. \par \par \uc2  "Ironically, given that a war is almost fully priced in, a negative report by (Chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans) Blix could have a more positive impact on the dollar than a negative one," UBS Warburg said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  That's because a negative report could compel the anti-war element in the Security Council to conclude that all options for a peaceful resolution to the crisis have been exhausted, forcing support for the U.S. hawkish stance, UBS Warburg said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP53.880 on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.940 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they were hesitant to sell the peso for fear the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep a bid under the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A trader with a local bank speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have sold around $40 million, mainly at the PHP53.950 level. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials weren't immediately available to comment. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended at its lowest level in five weeks. The dollar closed at 1,192.3 won, up from KRW1,183.8 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Offshore players were especially active Monday, with most traders saying the dollar buying was for hedging purposes. \par \par \uc2  Opinion was split over whether rising tensions between the U.S. and North Korea were having an impact on the won. \par \par \uc2  "Offshore players I've talked to have said that North Korea isn't going to have an impact because nothing has actually materialized, just threats," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened to its lowest level in four weeks, driven in part by local banks buying U.S. dollars in what was thought to be options-related trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7528, up from S$1.7433 late in Asia Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise also coincided with its climb against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slippage also weighed on the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.779, up from Friday's NT$34.757. \par \par \uc2  Weaker-than-expected trade data issued late Monday is expected to pressure the New Taiwan dollar when trading reopens Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The government announced that exports rose 4% in January, much lower than market expectations of an average 18% increase. \par \par \uc2  Corporate demand for U.S. dollars and Iraqi war fears added mild downward pressure to the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,880 rupiah, up from IDR8,870 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Investors fear that a war in Iraq could destabilize Indonesia - the world's most populous Muslim nation. Over the weekend, tens of thousands took to the streets in an anti-U.S. protest, but the event passed peacefully. \par \par \uc2  Dollar strength in the region prompted players to cover their short positions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the baht. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading the dollar was quoted at THB43.07, up from THB42.88 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  After opening in Asia around THB42.93, the dollar's early rise on offshore demand triggered short-covering at THB43.00, a foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Local oil importers joined the dollar buying in the afternoon, prompting more short-covering at THB43.05. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030210dz2a000l6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
