\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Won Ends At New Six-Month High \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,008 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2003\par 11:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won soared to a new six-month closing high Friday as a firmer yen set the tone for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar skidded under the weight of war concerns \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to just claw out of an intraday trough of 1,170.4 won to end at KRW1,170.8 won, down from KRW1,175.8 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said offshore players and a few exporters unloaded the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but overall trading was mostly quiet as market participants were wary that a stronger won would prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities. \par \par \uc2  "Some people believe that if the dollar/won goes below 1,170, the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  Frank Gong, Bank of America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Hong Kong, said the won's rise reflected global dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  "The yen/won rate has been relatively stable, so that tells you it's basically a dollar story and not a won story," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts warned of more downside to the dollar if market players become convinced that a war in Iraq is inevitable. \par \par \uc2  "Continued hawkish commentary from U.S. officials on Iraq remains the greenback's biggest bugbear, with the market not only concerned by the prospect of war itself, but much more worried by clear signs that the U.S. is prepared to go to war without the support of the rest of the U.N. Security Council," UBS Warburg said in a note. \par \par \uc2  With that in mind, market players will be keeping close tabs on several events next week, starting with Chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans Blix's report to the U.N. Security Council Monday on progress made in carrying out the resolution calling for Iraq to give up its weapons of mass destruction. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush's State of the Union address Tuesday will be closely followed, as will Treasury Secretary nominee John Snow's confirmation hearing the same day. Market players will be waiting to see if he says anything about the U.S.' strong dollar policy, though analysts say they don't expect him to stray from the official line. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Open Market Committee Meeting on Jan. 28-29 will also be closely watched, though most expect the Fed to stand pat on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts generally are convinced that the dollar will remain in the doldrums in the near term at the very least, which could keep the yen strengthening, and bolster Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Salomon Smith Barney/Citibank in a report Friday ratcheted up near term forecasts for the Indonesian rupiah, the South Korean won, the Singapore dollar, after revising upward targets for the yen. \par \par \uc2  But "in the second half of the year, we expect a number of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decouple from the yen, moving stronger," due to divergent fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "We expect Asia to accelerate along with the U.S. while Japan continues to stagnate," Salomon said, adding that the biggest move will likely be in the won. \par \par \uc2  At 0923 GMT, the dollar was quoted around Y117.84, down from Y118.06 overnight in New York, and Y118.73 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the yen's gains versus the dollar and foreign buying of local stocks helped support the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at NT$34.575, down from NT$34.658 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The intraday range was between NT$34.555 and NT$34.722, but it remained below NT$34.65 for much of the session. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar tracked the yen higher in thin trade ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of the local session, the dollar was quoted at S$1.7310, down from S$1.7326 late Thursday. Its intraday range was between S$1.7295 and S$1.7320. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar sentiment remained weak as market participants refrained from taking large positions amid continued concerns of a war in the Middle East, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But Singapore-based dealers weren't keen on pushing the local dollar up too high for fear of sparking \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Thai baht was just marginally higher as investors were wary of trying to build on recent gains, given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent dollar buying, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Market players were also reluctant to make more big bets on dollar weakness ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 42.68 baht, down a tad from THB42.69 Thursday, after trading between THB42.65 and THB42.70. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand said late Friday foreign reserves rose to $38.9 billion in the week to Jan. 17, from $38.4 billion a week earlier, confirming that it is continuing to buy dollars on weakness. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso firmed as banks took the cue from the yen's gains and reduced their holdings of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 53.590 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.665 Thursday, after moving in a range between PHP53.570 and PHP53.670. \par \par \uc2  Traders said banks opted to unload some of their dollar holdings after buying up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days amid concerns over the impact of a possible war in Iraq on the local economy. \par \par \uc2  "They can't push up the dollar because the dollar continues to weaken against the yen," said a trader with a local bank. "So they decided to square their positions." \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah ended just a tad higher amid talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of corporate demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local companies continued to buy dollars, possibly to hedge dollar obligations maturing later this year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia was suspected of selling dollars via state banks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 8,893 rupiah, only slightly down from IDR8,895 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030124dz1o000nx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
