\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Follow Yen Moves Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nirmala Menon \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2003\par 11:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuated within generally tight ranges Thursday, mostly following in the footsteps of the yen. \par \par \uc2  The session kicked off with most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening along with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid short-covering in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the yen later bounced back from session lows after Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa suggested in parliamentary questioning that Japan is no longer on red alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Regional units mirrored the yen's rebound, and by late Thursday, they were mixed against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, the Philippine peso and the Thai baht were modestly higher, while the Singapore dollar, the New Taiwan dollar and the Indonesian rupiah softened slightly. \par \par \uc2  Overall, underlying sentiment on the dollar remains bearish. A regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Singapore-based foreign bank said there was a lot of interest to sell dollars into rallies, but the number of buyers was limited. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen's fluctuations in recent days have confused many market players and "a lot of people are sidelined," the trader added. \par \par \uc2  At 0954 GMT, the dollar was quoted around Y118.16, from Y118.31 overnight in New York and Y118.19 in Tokyo late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "With the backdrop of poor U.S. data, Wall Street's struggles and war risks, it remains difficult picking a bottom for the U.S dollar," BNP Paribas wrote in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, heavy dollar selling by exporters and offshore players helped support the South Korean won, market participants said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1,175.8 won, down from KRW1,176.7 Wednesday, after trading between KRW1,175.2 and KRW1,180.7. \par \par \uc2  Sales from exporters were linked to traditional month-end book balancing, but dealers couldn't explain the offshore selling. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market was pretty active today," a dealer said. "There was lots of selling from offshore and exporters, and I think it followed the yen closely." \par \par \uc2  Lower corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the Philippine peso extend gains from the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 53.665 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP53.675 Wednesday, after moving between PHP53.650 and PHP53.750. Transaction volume dropped to $75.7 million from $90.5 million previously. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a dearth of fresh leads kept most market participants on the sidelines. A Manila-based dealer said trades were mostly from a handful of banks which took short-term positions for quick profits. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht firmed slightly as the dollar's early rebound lost steam toward the end of the session, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Exporter dollar-selling during the day also helped push the dollar lower amid a thin market," a trader at a local bank in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar erased early gains that took it to a high of 42.77 baht to end the local trading session at THB42.69 baht, down from THB42.74 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened, but came off its intraday trough, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to hang on to its early gains as market participants trimmed positions and took quick profits. \par \par \uc2  Toward the close of the local trading session, the U.S. dollar was quoted around S$1.7326, from S$1.7322 late Wednesday. Intraday, it traded between S$1.7320 and S$1.7352. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said worries that a U.S.-led war against Iraq was inevitable kept sentiment very bearish on the dollar in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the U.S. dollar to continue to drift lower toward end-week, with the U.S. dollar remaining at risk from another round of selling upon new adverse developments on the Iraqi war front," United Overseas Bank said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  But the local dollar's gains were limited by concerns over the domestic economic outlook and potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar weakened, but ended off-lows as exporter demand helped offset earlier short covering on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Sentiment on the local dollar was also aided by a 1.7% rise in the key stock index, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.658, mildly higher than Wednesday's NT$34.644, after trading between NT$34.651 and NT$35.765. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah drifted lower as local companies bought dollars, possibly to finance imports and to hedge future dollar obligations, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 8,895 rupiah, up from IDR8,885 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  State-owned oil company Pertamina was suspected of being the main dollar buyer for financing fuel imports, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia is the only South East Asian member of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, or OPEC. But, the country imports most of its fuel products for domestic use from Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Underlying sentiment remains positive on the local unit as offshore participants are still attracted by the healthy differentials between the rupiah and the dollar rates while political stability seems to improve, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030123dz1n000ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
