\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late; US Dlr Rise Sparks Short-Covering \b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2003\par 10:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar weakened Tuesday, after an early rebound of the U.S. dollar against the yen sparked short U.S. dollar covering in Asia, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0850 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7362, up from S$1.7326 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7307 and S$1.7363 intraday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.7305 and inched up through the Asian session after an early rebound against the yen sparked short U.S. dollar covering in Singapore, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose above S$1.7350 around midday, and followed up with an attempt to breach key underlying resistance at S$1.7360, sparked by its rise above Y119 against the yen. \par \par \uc2  After the initial attempt failed, U.S. dollar buying eased slightly, but resumed late in the Asian session, causing the resistance to eventually crumble, and driving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to its intraday high of S$1.7365. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar struggled to maintain its feeble grip and occasionally slipped back below S$1.7360, as sporadic selling emerged, as it found its hold above Y119 also slipping. \par \par \uc2  By the end of the Asian session, the U.S. dollar was changing hands at Y119.01, down from its session high of Y119.15. \par \par \uc2  Although ongoing concerns over the possibility of a U.S.-led war with Iraq continued to pressure the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , these were tempered by views that a rebound was overdue, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the uncertain outlook for the trade-dependent domestic economy, also helped fuel the rebound, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, most dealers say the U.S. dollar recovery isn't likely to hold, especially since it has been struggling to keep above S$1.7360, hinting that some participants were still seeking levels to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the U.S. dollar faces support at S$1.7330, and next resistance is expected at the psychological S$1.7400. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mixed late Tuesday, after profit taking emerged at the longer end of the curve while short bonds retained their early gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bonds had started mostly flat or modestly higher, but aggressive offers started to emerge around midmorning, mainly in the longer-term tenors, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  "There were good two-way flows in the market, but I think they are looking a bit toppish now. For example, looking at the 10-year bonds, the market may be a little uncomfortable with such (low) yields," a dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds closed two basis points lower at 1.06%, while the yield on five-year bonds was down three basis points at 1.68%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was one basis point higher at 2.11% while the yield on 10-year bonds gained three basis points to 2.59%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on 15-year bonds ended four basis points higher at 2.93%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swap offers were mostly higher, as\par they tracked some of the earlier rise in local bond yields. The two-year\par swap offer rose one basis point to 1.24%, while the five-year offer rose\par two basis points to 1.98%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 10-year offer ended one basis point higher at 2.66%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 144 basis points\par from 142 basis points late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the local money market, rates were mostly steady, as liquidity\par remained sufficient in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Tuesday      Late Monday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         0.4375% 0.5000%   0.5000% 0.5625%\par 3-Month           0.6875% 0.8125%   0.6875% 0.8125%\par 1-Year            0.8750% 1.0000%   0.8750% 1.0000%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154-157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030121dz1l000iq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
