\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lower Late; But Dlr Vulnerability Looms \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2003\par 11:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar staged a mild comeback against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday after a struggling in the region for most of the week. \par \par \uc2  Friday's gains illustrate how the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can benefit from occasional bouts of short covering. But analysts say the dollar will remain mired in a downward trend globally at least until the outcome of the confrontation with Iraq becomes clear. \par \par \uc2  Even then, the widening U.S. current account deficit will pose a major impediment to any chance for a sustained dollar recovery, they add. \par \par \uc2  Less than three weeks into the new year, the dollar is down 1.1% against the South Korean won, 0.8% against the New Taiwan dollar, 0.6% against the yen, and 0.3% against the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. dollar is under downward pressure and the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are facing upward pressure. This isn't a trend that will change anytime soon," said Geoffrey Barker, HSBC's chief economist for Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. will find it increasingly difficult to attract the huge flows of foreign capital it needs to fund its current account gap, thereby leaving the dollar vulnerable to further weakness, he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's decent performance against the yen set the tone for its gains against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday. Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  often track the direction of the yen as a reflex mechanism to preserve price competitiveness on trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded as high as Y118.17 during the Asian session. Late in the day it was quoted at Y117.99, up from Y117.83 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar came off a six-month high to trade slightly weaker late Friday. Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a disappointing trade report weighed on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Near the end of trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7334, up from S$1.7327 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's drop below the psychological S$1.7300 support level, hitting S$1.7290 in early trade, fueled fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The government reported that non-oil domestic exports, a key measurement of the trade-driven economy's health, rose a weaker-than-expected 4.4% in December from a year earlier. Analysts had been looking for a 13% rise. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar weakened for the first time in five trading sessions after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.467, up from Thursday's NT$34.450. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the New Taiwan dollar is likely to continue to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve next week, especially given poor outlook for U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a four-week high of PHP53.610 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.470 at Thursday's finish. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso lost ground from late morning as it tracked the losses in some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and due to modest corporate demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  The local unit extended its weaker performance through to the closing, with its losses exacerbated by some banks covering their short dollar positions ahead of the weekend, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean won, it edged lower after trading in a narrow range for most of the session. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,173.5 won, compared with KRW1,173.2 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  With few domestic developments to drive trading, the won largely tracked the yen's moves, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar short covering by local corporations helped keep a bid under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Thai baht. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at THB42.750, up from THB42.670 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian rupiah the dollar was unchanged at IDR8,785 after trading in a narrow range of IDR8,870 to IDR8,885. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Benjamin Pedley contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030117dz1h000lh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
